stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(every time he bartered his coats for a different kind of article; and) Tj T*
(there could be no current price, or regular quotations of value. Whereas) Tj T*
(now each thing has a current price in money, and he gets over all) Tj T*
(difficulties by reckoning his coat at L4 or L5, and a four-pound loaf at) Tj T*
(6_d._ or 7_d_. As it is much easier to compare different lengths by) Tj T*
(expressing them in a common language of feet and inches, so it is much) Tj T*
(easier to compare values by means of a common language of pounds,) Tj T*
(shillings, and pence. In no other way can values be arranged one above) Tj T*
(another in a scale: in no other can a person conveniently calculate the) Tj T*
(sum of his possessions; and it is easier to ascertain and remember the) Tj T*
(relations of many things to one thing, than their innumerable cross) Tj T*
(relations with one another. This advantage of having a common language) Tj T*
(in which values may be expressed, is, even by itself, so important, that) Tj T*
(some such mode of expressing and computing them would probably be used) Tj T*
(even if a pound or a shilling did not express any real thing, but a mere) Tj T*
(unit of calculation. It is said that there are African tribes in which) Tj T*
(this somewhat artificial contrivance actually prevails. They calculate) Tj T*
(the value of things in a sort of money of account, called macutes. They) Tj T*
(say, one thing is worth ten macutes, another fifteen, another twenty.) Tj T*
(There is no real thing called a macute: it is a conventional unit, for) Tj T*
(the more convenient comparison of things with one another.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This advantage, however, forms but an inconsiderable part of the) Tj T*
(economical benefits derived from the use of money. The inconveniences of) Tj T*
(barter are so great, that without some more commodious means of) Tj T*
(effecting exchanges, the division of employments could hardly have been) Tj T*
(carried to any considerable extent. A tailor, who had nothing but coats,) Tj T*
(might starve before he could find any person having bread to sell who) Tj T*
(wanted a coat: besides, he would not want as much bread at a time as) Tj T*
(would be worth a coat, and the coat could not be divided. Every person,) Tj T*
(therefore, would at all times hasten to dispose of his commodity in) Tj T*
(exchange for anything which, though it might not be fitted to his own) Tj T*
(immediate wants, was in great and general demand, and easily divisible,) Tj T*
(so that he might be sure of being able to purchase with it, whatever was) Tj T*
(offered for sale. The primary necessaries of life possess these) Tj T*
(properties in a high degree. Bread is extremely divisible, and an object) Tj T*
(of universal desire. Still, this is not the sort of thing required: for,) Tj T*
(of food, unless in expectation of a scarcity, no one wishes to possess) Tj T*
(more at once than is wanted for immediate consumption; so that a person) Tj T*
(is never sure of finding an immediate purchaser for articles of food;) Tj T*
(and unless soon disposed of, most of them perish. The thing which people) Tj T*
(would select to keep by them for making purchases, must be one which,) Tj T*
(besides being divisible, and generally desired, does not deteriorate by) Tj T*
(keeping. This reduces the choice to a small number of articles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By a tacit concurrence, almost all nations, at a very early period,) Tj T*
(fixed upon certain metals, and especially gold and silver, to serve this) Tj T*
(purpose. No other substances unite the necessary qualities in so great a) Tj T*
(degree, with so many subordinate advantages. Next to food and clothing,) Tj T*
(and in some climates even before clothing, the strongest inclination in) Tj T*
(a rude state of society is for personal ornament, and for the kind of) Tj T*
(distinction which is obtained by rarity or costliness in such ornaments.) Tj T*
ET
