(After the immediate necessities of life were satisfied, every one was) Tj T*
(eager to accumulate as great a store as possible of things at once) Tj T*
(costly and ornamental; which were chiefly gold, silver, and jewels.) Tj T*
(These were the things which it most pleased every one to possess, and) Tj T*
(which there was most certainty of finding others willing to receive in) Tj T*
(exchange for any kind of produce. They were among the most imperishable) Tj T*
(of all substances. They were also portable, and containing great value) Tj T*
(in small bulk, were easily hid; a consideration of much importance in an) Tj T*
(age of insecurity. Jewels are inferior to gold and silver in the quality) Tj T*
(of divisibility; and are of very various qualities, not to be accurately) Tj T*
(discriminated without great trouble. Gold and silver are eminently) Tj T*
(divisible, and when pure, always of the same quality; and their purity) Tj T*
(may be ascertained and certified by a public authority.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Accordingly, though furs have been employed as money in some countries,) Tj T*
(cattle in others, in Chinese Tartary cubes of tea closely pressed) Tj T*
(together, the shells called cowries on the coast of Western Africa, and) Tj T*
(in Abyssinia at this day blocks of rock salt; though even of metals, the) Tj T*
