(CHAPTER III) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(QUALITIES OF THE MATERIAL OF MONEY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([4]Many recent writers, such as Huskisson, MacCulloch, James Mill,) Tj T*
(Garnier, Chevalier, and Walras, have satisfactorily described the) Tj T*
(qualities which should be possessed by the material of money. Earlier) Tj T*
(writers seem, however, to have understood the subject almost as well....) Tj T*
(Of all writers, M. Chevalier ... probably gives the most accurate and) Tj T*
(full account of the properties which money should possess, and I shall) Tj T*
(in many points follow his views.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The prevailing defect in the treatment of the subject is the failure to) Tj T*
(observe that money requires different properties as regards different) Tj T*
(functions. To decide upon the best material for money is thus a problem) Tj T*
(of great complexity, because we must take into account at once the) Tj T*
(relative importance of the several functions of money, the degree in) Tj T*
(which money is employed for each function, and the importance of each of) Tj T*
(the physical qualities of the substance with respect to each function.) Tj T*
(In a simple state of industry money is chiefly required to pass about) Tj T*
(between buyers and sellers. It should, then, be conveniently portable,) Tj T*
(divisible into pieces of various size, so that any sum may readily be) Tj T*
(made up, and easily distinguishable by its appearance, or by the design) Tj T*
(impressed upon it. When money, however, comes to serve, as it will at) Tj T*
(some future time, almost exclusively as a measure and standard of value,) Tj T*
(the system of exchange, being one of perfected barter, such properties) Tj T*
(become a matter of comparative indifference, and stability of value,) Tj T*
(joined perhaps to portability, is the most important quality. Before) Tj T*
(venturing, however, to discuss such complex questions, we must proceed) Tj T*
(to a preliminary discussion of the properties in question, which may) Tj T*
(thus perhaps be enumerated in the order of their importance:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    1. Utility and value.) Tj T*
(    2. Portability.) Tj T*
(    3. Indestructibility.) Tj T*
(    4. Homogeneity.) Tj T*
(    5. Divisibility.) Tj T*
(    6. Stability of value.) Tj T*
(    7. Cognisability.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. UTILITY AND VALUE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Since money has to be exchanged for valuable goods, it should itself) Tj T*
(possess value and it must therefore have utility as the basis of value.) Tj T*
(Money, when once in full currency, is only received in order to be) Tj T*
(passed on, so that if all people could be induced to take worthless bits) Tj T*
(of material at a fixed rate of valuation, it might seem that money does) Tj T*
(not really require to have substantial value. Something like this does) Tj T*
