(PROVISIONS OF THE ACT OF 1890) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([24]The act of July 14, 1890, is[25] more remarkable than that of 1878.) Tj T*
(It is unique in monetary history. It provides that the Secretary of the) Tj T*
(Treasury shall purchase each month at the market price four and a half) Tj T*
(million ounces of silver bullion. In payment he shall issue Treasury) Tj T*
(notes of the United States, in denominations of between one dollar and) Tj T*
(one thousand dollars. These Treasury notes, unlike the old silver) Tj T*
(certificates, are a direct legal tender for all debts, public or) Tj T*
(private, unless a different medium is expressly stipulated in the) Tj T*
(contract. They differ from the silver certificates in another respect;) Tj T*
(they are redeemable either in gold or silver coin, at the discretion of) Tj T*
(the Secretary of the Treasury. The indirect process of redemption) Tj T*
(which,... was applied to the silver certificates, is replaced for the) Tj T*
(new notes by direct redemption. The avowed object is to keep the silver) Tj T*
(money equal to gold, for it is declared to be "the established policy of) Tj T*
(the United States to maintain the two metals at a parity with each other) Tj T*
(on the present legal ratio, or such ratio as may be provided by law.") Tj T*
(The act of 1878 is repealed; but the coinage of two million ounces of) Tj T*
(silver into dollars is to be continued for a year \(until July 1, 1891\).) Tj T*
(Thereafter it is directed that only so many silver dollars shall be) Tj T*
(coined as may be needed for redeeming any Treasury notes presented for) Tj T*
(redemption. Practically this means that the coinage shall cease;) Tj T*
(redemption in silver dollars will not be called for under present) Tj T*
(conditions. The coinage of silver dollars accordingly was suspended by) Tj T*
(the Treasury on July 1, 1891; a change which was the occasion of some) Tj T*
(vociferous abuse and equally vociferous praise, but which in reality was) Tj T*
(of no consequence whatever.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
