(deprecated independent action by any one nation. But the more) Tj T*
(thoroughgoing advocates of free silver in the United States say,) Tj T*
(certainly with much force, that an international agreement has proved to) Tj T*
(be simply impracticable, and that if the wider use of silver is to be) Tj T*
(deferred until there is concerted action by the great nations, it will) Tj T*
(never come. If anything in this direction is to be done, some one) Tj T*
(country must be courageous enough to take the lead, trusting that others) Tj T*
(will follow in due time. And certainly it is true that the scheme for) Tj T*
(international bimetallism has practically no prospect of adoption;) Tj T*
(while, on the other hand, the serious arguments urged by its advocates) Tj T*
(tell, in some degree, in favor of any scheme for enlarging the use of) Tj T*
(silver as money. These arguments, moreover, are of weight, and deserve a) Tj T*
(more painstaking consideration than is often admitted by those who) Tj T*
(oppose the silver legislation of the United States.) Tj T*
