(... Another important circumstance is the general transition in) Tj T*
(agricultural methods inevitable in those western states which have been) Tj T*
(settled for a generation or more. When new land is first taken into) Tj T*
(cultivation the most effective use of it is found in the continuous) Tj T*
(production of some staple crop like wheat and corn, which can be grown,) Tj T*
(so long as the cream of the soil is not exhausted, year after year with) Tj T*
(large returns. After a while, however, the land begins to show signs of) Tj T*
(exhaustion. The staple crops do not yield as largely as before, and less) Tj T*
(crude methods of using the soil must be resorted to. Manures have to be) Tj T*
(applied, and the rotation and selection of crops practised. Meat and) Tj T*
(dairy products, vegetables, fruits, and the miscellaneous agricultural) Tj T*
(articles, must take their place in rural economy. This change has been) Tj T*
(carried through very largely in states like New York, Pennsylvania, and) Tj T*
(Ohio. In the heart of the Mississippi Valley it is now under way; but) Tj T*
(the transition is trying, and to some of the farmers it is impossible. A) Tj T*
(good share of the American agricultural population has been so steadily) Tj T*
(bred to the easy and careless use of virgin soil that it cannot) Tj T*
(accommodate itself to more intensive methods. It is constantly moving) Tj T*
(westward; settling for a generation in one spot, and then, as the land) Tj T*
(shows signs of exhaustion, moving farther west. The more intelligent and) Tj T*
(versatile stay behind, adapt themselves to new conditions, and in time) Tj T*
(prosper under them. The least active also stay behind, and flounder) Tj T*
(hopelessly in the old ways. But a large number are always moving west.) Tj T*
(In every state between the Alleghanies and the Missouri river there are) Tj T*
(large tracts formerly cultivated by native settlers, who have sold their) Tj T*
(lands, as they showed signs of giving out, to German or Swedish) Tj T*
(immigrants. These latter have not infrequently paid good prices for the) Tj T*
(lands: but they have been bred to intensive farming, to careful and) Tj T*
(varied use of the soil, and they have prospered where their native) Tj T*
(predecessors have been unwilling or unable to adapt themselves to the) Tj T*
(new conditions. The period of transition is a hard one for all of the) Tj T*
(native farmers, whether they stay behind or move on, and the lesson of) Tj T*
(using the soil with more skill and care is learned only under the) Tj T*
(pressure of necessity. In such periods all sorts of remedies for hard) Tj T*
(times make their appearance and have their run.) Tj T*
