(By far the most important ... consequence ... [of the Restriction Act]) Tj T*
(is, that while the convertibility into specie no longer exists as a) Tj T*
(check to an over-issue of paper, the Bank Directors have not perceived) Tj T*
(that the removal of that check rendered it possible that such an excess) Tj T*
(might be issued by the discount of perfectly good bills. So far from) Tj T*
(perceiving this ... they maintain the contrary doctrine with the utmost) Tj T*
(confidence.... That this doctrine is a very fallacious one, your) Tj T*
(Committee cannot entertain a doubt. The fallacy, upon which it is) Tj T*
(founded, lies in not distinguishing between an advance of capital to) Tj T*
(merchants, and an addition of supply of currency to the general mass of) Tj T*
(circulating medium. If the advance of capital only is considered, as) Tj T*
(made to those who are ready to employ it in judicious and productive) Tj T*
(undertakings, it is evident there need be no other limit to the total) Tj T*
(amount of advances than what the means of the lender, and his prudence) Tj T*
(in the selection of borrowers, may impose. But in the present situation) Tj T*
(of the Bank, intrusted as it is with the function of supplying the) Tj T*
(public with that paper currency which forms the basis of our) Tj T*
(circulation, and at the same time not subjected to the liability of) Tj T*
(converting the paper into specie, every advance which it makes of) Tj T*
(capital to the merchants in the shape of discount, becomes an addition) Tj T*
(also to the mass of circulating medium. In the first instance, when the) Tj T*
(advance is made by notes paid in discount of a bill, it is undoubtedly) Tj T*
(so much capital, so much power of making purchases, placed in the hands) Tj T*
(of the merchant who receives the notes; and if those hands are safe, the) Tj T*
(operation is so far, and in this its first step, useful and productive) Tj T*
(to the public. But as soon as the portion of circulating medium in which) Tj T*
(the advance was thus made performs in the hands of him to whom it was) Tj T*
(advanced this its first operation as capital, as soon as the notes are) Tj T*
(exchanged by him for some other article which is capital, they fall into) Tj T*
(the channel of circulation as so much circulating medium, and form an) Tj T*
(addition to the mass of currency. The necessary effect of every such) Tj T*
(addition to the mass is to diminish the relative value of any given) Tj T*
(portion of that mass in exchange for commodities. If the addition were) Tj T*
(made by notes convertible into specie, this diminution of the relative) Tj T*
(value of any given portion of the whole mass would speedily bring back) Tj T*
(upon the Bank which issued the notes as much as was excessive. But if by) Tj T*
(law they are not so convertible, of course this excess will not be) Tj T*
(brought back, but will remain in the channel of circulation, until paid) Tj T*
(in again to the Bank itself in discharge of the bills which were) Tj T*
(originally discounted. During the whole time they remain out, they) Tj T*
(perform all the functions of circulating medium; and before they come to) Tj T*
(be paid in discharge of those bills, they have already been followed by) Tj T*
(a new issue of notes in a similar operation of discounting. Each) Tj T*
(successive advance repeats the same process. If the whole sum of) Tj T*
(discounts continues outstanding at a given amount, there will remain) Tj T*
(permanently out in circulation a corresponding amount of paper; and if) Tj T*
(the amount of discounts is progressively increasing, the amount of) Tj T*
(paper, which remains out in circulation over and above what is otherwise) Tj T*
(wanted for the occasions of the public, will progressively increase) Tj T*
(also, and the money prices of commodities will progressively rise. This) Tj T*
(progress may be as indefinite as the range of speculation and adventure) Tj T*
