(Two facts normally give deposits a more or less definite ratio to money.) Tj T*
(The first ... [is] that bank reserves are kept in a more or less) Tj T*
(definite ratio to bank deposits. The second is that individuals, firms,) Tj T*
(and corporations preserve more or less definite ratios between their) Tj T*
(cash transactions and their check transactions, and also between their) Tj T*
(money and deposit balances.[49] These ratios are determined by motives) Tj T*
(of individual convenience and habit. In general, business firms use) Tj T*
(money for wage payments, and for small miscellaneous transactions) Tj T*
(included under the term "petty cash"; while for settlements with each) Tj T*
(other they usually prefer checks. These preferences are so strong that) Tj T*
(we could not imagine them overridden except temporarily and to a small) Tj T*
(degree. A business firm would hardly pay car fares with checks and) Tj T*
(liquidate its large liabilities with cash. Each person strikes an) Tj T*
(equilibrium between his use of the two methods of payment, and does not) Tj T*
(greatly disturb it except for short periods of time. He keeps his stock) Tj T*
(of money or his bank balance in constant adjustment to the payments he) Tj T*
(makes in money or by check. Whenever his stock of money becomes) Tj T*
(relatively small and his bank balance relatively large, he cashes a) Tj T*
(check. In the opposite event, he deposits cash. In this way he is) Tj T*
(constantly converting one of the two media of exchange into the other. A) Tj T*
(private individual usually feeds his purse from his bank account; a) Tj T*
(retail commercial firm usually feeds its bank account from its till. The) Tj T*
(bank acts as intermediary for both.) Tj T*
