([80]When the Government of British India sought, in 1893, to give a) Tj T*
(fixed gold value to about L120,000,000 in rupee silver, it undertook an) Tj T*
(experiment of great importance to the financial world, and one which was) Tj T*
(naturally viewed in many quarters with grave misgivings. The experience) Tj T*
(of fifteen years which have followed that experiment has taught many) Tj T*
(lessons in monetary science. It may, indeed, be said to have blazed a) Tj T*
(new path in the principles of money--at least, in their practical) Tj T*
(application. The effort to raise the coins to a fixed gold value by) Tj T*
(scarcity alone was not successful, but it led to other devices, which,) Tj T*
(imitated or improved upon in Mexico, the Philippines, and the Straits) Tj T*
(Settlements, as well as in India, have created a new type of monetary) Tj T*
(system which has come to bear the title of the gold exchange standard.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The gold exchange standard differs in several respects from the limping) Tj T*
(standard. It has been the product of definite purpose and plan in the) Tj T*
(Philippines and in Mexico and to a certain extent in India. While in) Tj T*
(British India it has been, like the limping standard, a compromise with) Tj T*
(existing conditions, it has there, as elsewhere, received a definite) Tj T*
(form and substance which separated it from the limping standard as) Tj T*
(evolved in France and in other countries which found themselves with a) Tj T*
(large amount of legal-tender silver on their hands when the metal had) Tj T*
(fallen below the official parity. There are two other essential) Tj T*
(differences between the limping standard and the gold exchange standard.) Tj T*
(One is that the gold exchange standard contemplates a circulation of) Tj T*
(token coins of silver without any necessary concurrent circulation of) Tj T*
(gold or paper. The other is that the gold exchange standard contemplates) Tj T*
(definite and comprehensive measures to maintain the value of token coins) Tj T*
(at par with gold instead of relying purely upon custom and scarcity to) Tj T*
(give them value.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The essential principle upon which the exchange standard has been) Tj T*
(established is that the value of money is governed by the law of supply) Tj T*
(and demand. So long as supply was indefinite and excessive, as under the) Tj T*
(system of the free coinage of silver, there was no way of preventing) Tj T*
(safely and effectively the decline in the gold value of the coins to the) Tj T*
(bullion value of their silver contents. The moment, however, that) Tj T*
(Government undertook to limit the supply of coins to the demand for) Tj T*
(them, it took an important step to separate their value from that of) Tj T*
(their bullion contents and to give them a value based upon the demand) Tj T*
(for them as money signs required for carrying on exchanges. Strangely) Tj T*
(enough, while this principle had been in operation for many years in the) Tj T*
(case of subsidiary coins, its bearing upon the use of silver in) Tj T*
(countries where the standard had been depreciating was not clearly) Tj T*
(comprehended until within recent years. Those who understood the) Tj T*
(principle doubted its sufficiency to give a fixed value to silver coins) Tj T*
(as the sole medium of exchange, or they distrusted the ability of any) Tj T*
(government to judge accurately the number of coins required.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Upon the latter point they would have been correct if dependence had) Tj T*
(been placed upon guesswork or any empirical method of determining the) Tj T*
