(amount needed. It remained to find the true solution of the problem by) Tj T*
(so regulating the quantity of the coins that it would respond) Tj T*
(automatically to the demands of trade. The correct method of doing this) Tj T*
(is through the system of exchange funds. As this system is operated in) Tj T*
(the Philippines, it is not possible to obtain gold coin for silver) Tj T*
(certificates in small quantities; but it is possible always to obtain) Tj T*
(drafts upon New York at par, plus the usual charges for exchange between) Tj T*
(gold standard countries. These drafts have to be purchased with actual) Tj T*
(silver coin or coin certificates. In either case the coins and) Tj T*
(certificates are, by the requirements of the coinage law, held in the) Tj T*
(Philippine Treasury. The law does not permit their deposit by the) Tj T*
(Treasury in current account at a bank, which would turn them back into) Tj T*
(the general circulation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For practical purposes the volume of currency in circulation is) Tj T*
(contracted to the same extent as if a corresponding amount of gold were) Tj T*
(taken from the circulation for export. When the current turns and rates) Tj T*
(for money become high in the Philippines, Philippine currency can be) Tj T*
(released for local circulation by the purchase in New York from the gold) Tj T*
(standard fund of bills upon the Philippine Treasury. This rule of) Tj T*
(locking up the proceeds of the sale of bills is not rigidly applied to) Tj T*
(the funds in New York, because the influence of the Philippine purchases) Tj T*
(upon the local circulation there would be insignificant. On the) Tj T*
(contrary, the Government obtains a generous interest rate, which has at) Tj T*
(times been as high as 4 per cent., upon the deposit of Philippine funds) Tj T*
(with New York bankers. During the stress of the autumn of 1907) Tj T*
(considerable transfers of capital were made from Manila to New York by) Tj T*
(means of the purchase of New York drafts from the Philippine Treasury.) Tj T*
(The process, often repeated even under less serious pressure, clearly) Tj T*
(shows that the monetary system of the Philippines is linked to gold, and) Tj T*
(that capital can be freely transferred upon a gold basis between Manila) Tj T*
(and other markets.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The experience of fifteen years since the free coinage of rupees was) Tj T*
(first suspended in British India, of five years since the new system was) Tj T*
(established in the Philippines, and of nearly four years since it was in) Tj T*
(operation in Mexico, have settled most of the doubts which were felt) Tj T*
(when the experiment was undertaken in India. In the first place, it has) Tj T*
(been made clear that the value of the coins in exchange, as fixed by) Tj T*
(law, has not been influenced by variations in the price of silver) Tj T*
(bullion. This statement, of course, applies only to one side of the) Tj T*
(problem--the fall of the gold value of the silver in the coin below its) Tj T*
(face value. It would not be possible under any system yet discovered,) Tj T*
(except such uneconomic devices as prohibiting exportation, to prevent) Tj T*
(the disappearance of silver coins when the [bullion] value of their) Tj T*
(contents rises above the legal value in exchange. Both the Philippines) Tj T*
(and Mexico have faced this menace to their monetary circulation since) Tj T*
(their systems were inaugurated, but both have succeeded in removing it.) Tj T*
(In the Philippines the contents of the silver unit--the peso--was) Tj T*
(reduced in 1906 from about 371 grains to 247 grains in pure silver. The) Tj T*
(amount fixed by the law of 1903 was practically the same as the contents) Tj T*
(of the old Mexican dollar. The adoption of a coin of this weight was) Tj T*
