($71,646,500 \(about L7,200,000\).[81] The gold has gone chiefly into the) Tj T*
(reserves of the banks, which have in circulation about $95,000,000 in) Tj T*
(notes. Gold holdings of the banks, which were only $15,832,840 in) Tj T*
(January, 1906, were $54,165,483 in October, 1907, while silver holdings) Tj T*
(declined over the same period from $49,781,155 to $14,399,924.[82] This) Tj T*
(influx of gold came about because silver at 33 pence was above the) Tj T*
(Mexican coinage ratio of about 32 to 1, and much of it was sold by the) Tj T*
(Commission on Money and Exchange at a direct profit to the Mexican) Tj T*
(Treasury. In view of the subsequent fall in silver below 23 pence, at) Tj T*
(which rate Mexico is in a position to replenish her supply of subsidiary) Tj T*
(coinage, her statesmen may claim the credit of following the great rule) Tj T*
(of profit in the commercial world as well as on the stock exchange--to) Tj T*
(sell when things are dear and to buy when things are cheap.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The coincidence in the rise of silver and the adoption of the Mexican) Tj T*
(monetary reform in 1905 was in some degree accidental. It facilitated) Tj T*
(the reform, not only by introducing gold, but by removing the objections) Tj T*
(which would otherwise have been heard from the miners of silver to the) Tj T*
(rise in gold wages which would have accompanied a fixing of the exchange) Tj T*
(at a point above the value of silver bullion. It was the intention of) Tj T*
(the Mexican Government, however, to proceed resolutely, though) Tj T*
(deliberately, to a fixed exchange, and they would undoubtedly have) Tj T*
