() Tj T*
(The most serious difficulty which has been foreseen by critics of the) Tj T*
(gold exchange system relates to the sufficiency of the exchange funds.) Tj T*
(Up to the period of the general panic of 1907 and the crop failure in) Tj T*
(India in the spring of 1908, it might fairly be said, perhaps, that the) Tj T*
(system had not been subjected to any but "fair weather" conditions. The) Tj T*
(experience of India, however, has thrown striking light upon the) Tj T*
(possibilities and limitations of the system in time of stress. The test) Tj T*
(in India has been of such magnitude, moreover, that its results are much) Tj T*
(more conclusive than any test which might have been afforded in a) Tj T*
(smaller country dealing with a less enormous mass of token coins. If the) Tj T*
(test had come before the exchange funds had acquired a respectable size,) Tj T*
(the system might have been allowed to break down, through timidity and) Tj T*
(delay in taking proper measures of protection, and discredit have thus) Tj T*
(been cast upon it before it had been fairly tried.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What happened in India was that the failure of the crops deprived the) Tj T*
(country of the usual means of compensating by exports the heavy imports) Tj T*
(of foreign goods which had been contracted for. It became necessary,) Tj T*
(under the settled principles of exchange, to find gold to fill the gap.) Tj T*
(Usually the exchange account substantially balanced itself by the sale) Tj T*
(in London of Council drafts upon the Indian Government to obtain gold to) Tj T*
(pay the interest on the debt held in England. These drafts were) Tj T*
(purchased by importers in London, and used to pay for the Indian crops;) Tj T*
(but all through the spring of 1908 purchasers for drafts failed to) Tj T*
(appear, because there had been no considerable exports of Indian crops) Tj T*
(to be paid for. Hence Council drafts were without a market, and for a) Tj T*
(moment it seemed that the link which bound the Indian monetary system to) Tj T*
(the gold market of London had been severed, and that the silver rupee) Tj T*
(might drop as disastrously as the Mexican dollar before its free coinage) Tj T*
(was suspended. This would have added the influence of an appalling) Tj T*
(disaster to the burden already imposed upon Indian finance by the) Tj T*
(failure of the crops, for it would have compelled the Indian importer of) Tj T*
(English goods to find a greatly increased number of rupees to meet his) Tj T*
(gold obligations in London. Obviously, it was a disaster which, if it) Tj T*
(had occurred, would have invited the bankruptcy of the country,) Tj T*
(reflected lasting disgrace upon English financial foresight, and perhaps) Tj T*
(even have led to organised revolt.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Indian Government had available for meeting the crisis about) Tj T*
(L18,500,000, principally invested in securities in London. This fund,) Tj T*
(known as the gold standard reserve, was distinct from the currency) Tj T*
(reserve, consisting of gold received for currency notes, which amounted) Tj T*
(in the spring of 1908 to about L12,000,000. It was against the former) Tj T*
(fund that the Indian Government felt compelled to offer to sell exchange) Tj T*
(in India. Such offers were made for a time in limited amounts of) Tj T*
(L500,000 each, but they proved substantially adequate for meeting the) Tj T*
(demand, and by early summer the demand fell below the supply. The offer) Tj T*
(of exchange in this form for rupees maintained the value of the rupee) Tj T*
