([116]Complete description of the various forms of activity of the) Tj T*
(foreign exchange department of an important firm would fill a large) Tj T*
(volume, but there are certain stock operations in foreign exchange which) Tj T*
(are the basis of most of the transactions carried out and the) Tj T*
(understanding of which ought to go a long way toward making clear what) Tj T*
(the nature of the foreign exchange department's business really is.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I. SELLING "DEMAND" AGAINST "DEMAND") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first and most elementary form of activity is, of course, the buying) Tj T*
(of demand bills at a certain price and the selling of the banker's own) Tj T*
(demand drafts against them at a higher price. A banker finds, for) Tj T*
(instance, that he can buy John Smith & Co.'s sight draft for L1,000, on) Tj T*
(London, at the rate of 4.86, and that he can sell his own draft for) Tj T*
(L1,000 on his London banking correspondent at 4.87. All he has to do,) Tj T*
(therefore, is to buy John Smith's draft for $4,860, send it to London) Tj T*
(for credit of his account there, and then draw his own draft for L1,000) Tj T*
(on the newly created balance, selling it for $4,870. It cost him $4,860) Tj T*
(to buy the commercial draft, and he has sold his own draft against it) Tj T*
(for $4,870. His gross profit on the transaction, therefore, is $10.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As may be imagined, not very much money is made in transactions exactly) Tj T*
(of this kind--the one cited is taken only because it illustrates the) Tj T*
(principle. For whether the banker sends over in every mail a bewildering) Tj T*
(assortment of every conceivable form of foreign exchange to be credited) Tj T*
(to his account abroad, or whether he confines himself to remittances of) Tj T*
(the simplest kind of bills, the idea remains exactly the same--he is) Tj T*
(depositing money to the credit of his account in order that he may have) Tj T*
(a balance on which he can draw. That is, indeed, the sum and substance) Tj T*
(of the exchange business of the foreign department of most banking) Tj T*
(houses--the maintaining of deposit accounts in banks at foreign centres) Tj T*
(on which deposit account the bank here is in a position to draw) Tj T*
(according to the wants and needs of its customers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(II. SELLING CABLES AGAINST DEMAND EXCHANGE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A "cable," so-called, differs from a sight draft only in that the banker) Tj T*
(abroad who is to pay out the money is advised to do so by means of a) Tj T*
(telegraphic message instead of by a bit of paper instructing him to "pay) Tj T*
(to the order of so and so.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
