(London for the value of her imports, and the principal reason for this) Tj T*
(procedure is that the manufacturer in Great Britain does not wish to be) Tj T*
(bothered with the variations in exchange, although as the reader has) Tj T*
(seen, he may be pretty severely affected if silver has depreciated) Tj T*
(before his goods are sold. Leaving that out of the question, however, we) Tj T*
(may take it that as all his expenses are payable in gold, he naturally) Tj T*
(prefers to deal in terms of that metal. Consequently, goods shipped to) Tj T*
(China are nearly always paid for by remittances, or drawn for in) Tj T*
(sterling, which comes to the same thing. The Chinese producer is on) Tj T*
(rather a different footing. His expenses are in silver, and in silver he) Tj T*
(wishes to be paid. His produce, however, he has sold to Great Britain) Tj T*
(for a gold price, and either he cannot afford to, or does not want to) Tj T*
(wait until a remittance can be sent by mail from London. The one way) Tj T*
(open to him is to draw in sterling and settle the rate of exchange on) Tj T*
(the spot, which he does and so makes an end of the matter....) Tj T*
