(     The following discussion of clearing houses is confined) Tj T*
(     mainly to the United States and England. References to the) Tj T*
(     clearing houses of France and Germany, where the) Tj T*
(     introduction of the use of checks and the consequent) Tj T*
(     development of clearing facilities have been tardy, are) Tj T*
(     contained in the chapters devoted to the banking systems of) Tj T*
(     those countries.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I. IN THE UNITED STATES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A CLEARING HOUSE DEFINED) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([121]What is a clearing house? The Supreme Court of the State of) Tj T*
(Pennsylvania has defined it thus:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     It is an ingenious device to simplify and facilitate the) Tj T*
(     work of the banks in reaching an adjustment and payment of) Tj T*
(     the daily balances due to and from each other at one time) Tj T*
(     and in one place on each day. In practical operation it is a) Tj T*
(     place where all the representatives of the banks in a given) Tj T*
(     city meet, and, under the supervision of a competent) Tj T*
(     committee or officer selected by the associated banks,) Tj T*
(     settle their accounts with each other and make or receive) Tj T*
(     payment of balances and so "clear" the transactions of the) Tj T*
(     day for which the settlement is made.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But we must go farther than this, for though originally designed as a) Tj T*
(labor-saving device, the clearing house has expanded far beyond those) Tj T*
(limits, until it has become a medium for united action among the banks) Tj T*
(in ways that did not exist even in the imagination of those who were) Tj T*
(instrumental in its inception. A clearing house, therefore, may be) Tj T*
(defined as a device to simplify and facilitate the daily exchanges of) Tj T*
(items and settlements of balances among the banks and a medium for) Tj T*
(united action upon all questions affecting their mutual welfare.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(METHODS OF EXCHANGE IN NEW YORK PRIOR TO 1853) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([122]During a comparatively short period immediately following 1849 the) Tj T*
(number of banks in New York increased from 24 to 60. In the daily course) Tj T*
(of business each bank received checks and other items on each of the) Tj T*
(other banks, which had to be presented for collection. All such items on) Tj T*
(hand were assorted and listed on separate slips at the close of the day,) Tj T*
(and items coming in through the mail on the following morning were added) Tj T*
(at that time. To make the daily exchanges each bank sent out a porter) Tj T*
(with a book of entry, or pass book, together with the items to be) Tj T*
(exchanged.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The receiving teller of the first bank visited entered the exchanges) Tj T*
ET
