(brought by the porter on the credit side of his book and the return) Tj T*
(exchanges on the debit side, who then hurried away to deliver and) Tj T*
(receive in like manner at the other banks. It often happened that five) Tj T*
(or six porters would meet at the same bank, thereby retarding one) Tj T*
(another's progress and causing much delay. Considerable time was) Tj T*
(consumed in making the circuit. Hence, the entry of the return items in) Tj T*
(the books of the several banks was delayed until the afternoon, at an) Tj T*
(hour when the other work of the bank was becoming urgent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A daily settlement of the balances was not attempted by the banks, owing) Tj T*
(to the time it would have required, but they informally agreed upon a) Tj T*
(weekly adjustment, the same to take place after the exchanges on Friday) Tj T*
(morning. At that time the cashier of each bank drew a check for each of) Tj T*
(the several balances due it, and sent a porter out to collect them. At) Tj T*
(the same time the porter carried coin with which to pay balances due by) Tj T*
(his bank. After the settlement had been made, there was a meeting to) Tj T*
(adjust differences and bring order out of chaos.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An old bank officer \(J. S. Gibbons\), in describing the inconveniences) Tj T*
(and defects of this system, says that some of the more speculative banks) Tj T*
(took advantage of the weekly method of settlements by carrying a line of) Tj T*
(discounts to an amount greater than their legitimate resources would) Tj T*
(allow. Thus, a bank would manage to carry a small debit balance of) Tj T*
($2,000 or $3,000 with thirty or more institutions, making a total debit) Tj T*
(balance of, say, $100,000 on which it discounted paper. It was the) Tj T*
(practice to borrow enough on Thursday to make the settlements on Friday,) Tj T*
(and the return of the loan on Saturday threw it again into the debtor) Tj T*
(column. Virtually, therefore, the weekly settlements were nominal only,) Tj T*
(and to show that there was no attempt at economy of time and labor in) Tj T*
(making them, it is only necessary to say that the cashier drew a check) Tj T*
(for every balance due him, whereas a draft on one bank in favor of) Tj T*
(another might have settled two accounts at once.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The banks were at liberty to draw on each other for their credit) Tj T*
(balances without waiting for the settlements on Friday, and hence, when) Tj T*
(specie was needed, this was not infrequently done. But so far did many) Tj T*
(of the banks extend their loans and discounts that a single small draft) Tj T*
(by one bank on another would induce a general drawing and involve them) Tj T*
(all in confusion and virtual war on each other. Three o'clock would) Tj T*
(arrive, with the line of drafts incomplete, thus enabling debtor banks) Tj T*
(ofttimes to add $50,000 to their specie, whereas creditor banks would) Tj T*
(find themselves at the close of the day depleted in perhaps twice that) Tj T*
(sum.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE ORIGIN OF THE NEW YORK CLEARING HOUSE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([123]The desirability of a substitute for such a system had long been) Tj T*
(realized, but as yet no plausible scheme had been proposed. As early as) Tj T*
(1831 a plan had been suggested by Albert Gallatin, which, to a very) Tj T*
(remarkable degree, coincided with the one ultimately adopted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
