(But the times were not ripe for the scheme thus proposed. Mr. Gallatin) Tj T*
(was thinking in advance of the age. In time, however, the question began) Tj T*
(to be more generally discussed. For nearly a year it was under) Tj T*
(consideration, and finally it was deemed advisable to call a meeting to) Tj T*
(take decisive action upon it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On August 23, 1853, 16 presidents, 1 vice-president, and 21 cashiers,) Tj T*
(representing 38 banks, assembled in the directors' room of the) Tj T*
(Merchants' Bank, and at this meeting a resolution was passed providing) Tj T*
(that "a committee be appointed to procure or hire a suitable room in or) Tj T*
(near Wall Street, for the purpose of holding meetings of the officers of) Tj T*
(the city banks; that the said committee be requested to submit a plan,) Tj T*
(at an adjourned meeting of this body, to simplify the system of making) Tj T*
(exchanges and settling the daily balances; and that when a room is) Tj T*
(procured or hired for the above purpose, the presidents or cashiers be) Tj T*
(requested to meet weekly until a plan is agreed upon." In compliance) Tj T*
(with this request, the committee presented a plan for the daily) Tj T*
(settlement of balances, at a meeting held on August 31, 1853, which plan) Tj T*
(was amended so as to provide "that a room be procured for that purpose,) Tj T*
(sufficiently large to afford suitable accommodations.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On September 13, 1853, the scheme was adopted and the committee was) Tj T*
("clothed with full power to hire a room, appoint a manager and clerks,) Tj T*
(and make all the necessary arrangements to carry the plan for a clearing) Tj T*
(house into effect." The date for beginning operations was fixed for) Tj T*
(October 11. Accordingly, on the appointed day, the representatives of) Tj T*
(the banks, members of the association, met in a room which had been) Tj T*
(procured in the basement at No. 14 Wall Street, and made the first) Tj T*
(exchanges. The total clearings on that day were $22,648,109.87, and the) Tj T*
(balances were $1,290,572.38. These clearings have since been eclipsed by) Tj T*
(over $30,000,000 in the totals of a single bank.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The clearing system in America was thus fairly launched, and from that) Tj T*
(time forth its success exceeded the expectations of even its most ardent) Tj T*
(projectors. The association consisted at that time of 52 banks, banded) Tj T*
(together for their common good, which, as they then conceived, consisted) Tj T*
(solely in the exchange of items and settlement of balances at a uniform) Tj T*
(time and place. For nearly a year the operations were conducted without) Tj T*
(a constitution. The adoption of such an instrument was opposed, on the) Tj T*
(ground that it was not needed and might lead to a dangerous) Tj T*
(concentration of power in the hands of a few managers, who might use it) Tj T*
(for personal aggrandizement, or for the exercise of an arbitrary) Tj T*
(supervision.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(MEMBERSHIP AND ADMITTANCE FEES AT NEW YORK) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([124]The association at present \(1909\) consists of 50 members[125] \(32) Tj T*
(National Banks and 18 State Banks\) and the United States subtreasury) Tj T*
(located at New York. The latter makes its exchanges only at the clearing) Tj T*
(house, its balances being settled at its own counter. It has no voice in) Tj T*
(the government of the association, and pays a nominal sum for actual) Tj T*
