(expenses. The privilege which the subtreasury enjoys of making its) Tj T*
(exchanges through the clearing house is a matter of great accommodation) Tj T*
(both to the subtreasury and to the banks. The New York post-office) Tj T*
(clears through one of the members, but renders no compensation to the) Tj T*
(association for the privilege.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The membership of the association since its organization has been) Tj T*
(constantly changing, owing to the admission and expulsion of members and) Tj T*
(voluntary withdrawals, as provided by the constitution.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The association began with 51 members, but by 1858 the list had declined) Tj T*
(to 46, the lowest number in the history of the clearing house. A) Tj T*
(membership of 67 was attained in 1895.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On February 28, 1854, the Bank of the Union was expelled and the) Tj T*
(clearing-house association was authorized to return to it whatever) Tj T*
(amount was necessary to offset its advances toward the expenses of the) Tj T*
(clearing house. In the following December the Empire City Bank was) Tj T*
(expelled and a similar resolution was passed but in no case thereafter) Tj T*
(were any such refunds made....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The constitution is very explicit in its terms governing the admission) Tj T*
(and conduct of members. Applicants are first considered by the) Tj T*
(clearing-house committee and referred hence to the committee on) Tj T*
(admissions. The latter committee, if, in its opinion, after a careful) Tj T*
(examination, the applicants are qualified for membership, refers them to) Tj T*
(the association for final action, a three-fourths vote of those present) Tj T*
(being necessary for admission. Banks may be elected to membership at any) Tj T*
(meeting of the association, but before being considered by the) Tj T*
(clearing-house committee each applicant must be shown to have an) Tj T*
(unimpaired capital or an unimpaired capital and surplus of at least) Tj T*
($500,000. Each new member is required to signify its assent to the) Tj T*
(constitution, in the same manner as the original members, and pay an) Tj T*
(admission fee, according to capital, as follows: A bank the capital of) Tj T*
(which does not exceed $5,000,000 must pay $5,000; a bank the capital of) Tj T*
(which exceeds $5,000,000 must pay $7,500. Any member increasing its) Tj T*
(capital is required to pay in accordance with those rates.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
([126]METHODS OF SETTLING BALANCES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are no less than five different methods of settling balances, in) Tj T*
(whole or in part, without the use of money at the clearing house. They) Tj T*
(are \(1\) by manager's check on debtor banks given to creditor banks; \(2\)) Tj T*
(by borrowing and loaning balances without interest; \(3\) by borrowing and) Tj T*
(loaning balances with interest; \(4\) by the use of one or more of four) Tj T*
(forms of certificates, viz., gold and currency depository certificates,) Tj T*
(United States assistant treasurer certificates, and clearing-house loan) Tj T*
(certificates; and \(5\) by draft on another city.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When money is not used in the adjustment of balances at the clearing) Tj T*
(house, one of the most common methods of settlement is by manager's) Tj T*
ET
