(Two fairly distinct powers were given the clearing-house committee: the) Tj T*
(right to issue clearing-house certificates, and control over the) Tj T*
(currency portion of the reserves of the banks. This machinery was) Tj T*
(devised \(according to tradition\) after the crisis of 1857 by George S.) Tj T*
(Coe, who for more than thirty years was president of the American) Tj T*
(Exchange National Bank. The purpose of the certificate was to remove) Tj T*
(certain serious difficulties which had become generally recognized) Tj T*
(during that crisis. The banks had pursued a policy of loan contraction) Tj T*
(which ultimately led to general suspension, because it had proved) Tj T*
(impossible to secure any agreement among them.[131] The banks which were) Tj T*
(prepared to assist the business community with loans could not do so) Tj T*
(because they would be certain to be found with unfavorable) Tj T*
(clearing-house balances in favor of the banks which followed a more) Tj T*
(selfish course. The loan certificate provided a means of payment other) Tj T*
(than cash. What was more important, it took away the temptation from any) Tj T*
(single bank to seek to strengthen itself at the expense of its fellows,) Tj T*
(and rendered each bank more willing to assist the community with loans) Tj T*
(to the extent of its power.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But in addition to the arrangement for the use of loan certificates) Tj T*
(provision was also made for what was called the equalization of) Tj T*
(reserves. The individual banks were not, of course, equally strong in) Tj T*
(reserves at the times when loan certificates were authorized. From that) Tj T*
(moment they would be unable to strengthen themselves, aside from the) Tj T*
