(CHAPTER XX) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(STATE BANKS AND TRUST COMPANIES SINCE THE PASSAGE OF THE NATIONAL BANK) Tj T*
(ACT) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
([140]The banking institutions of the United States other than national) Tj T*
(banks are ordinarily classified into \(a\) state banks, \(b\) trust) Tj T*
(companies, \(c\) stock savings banks, \(d\) mutual savings banks, and \(e\)) Tj T*
(private banks. The following pages deal with two of these classes, viz.,) Tj T*
(state banks and trust companies. It will be desirable at the outset to) Tj T*
(distinguish them from the other classes, and to outline the history of) Tj T*
(legislation concerning them since 1865.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The term "state bank" has been used in the United States in several) Tj T*
(different senses; but whatever the variance in meaning, such banks have) Tj T*
(always had one common characteristic--incorporation under state) Tj T*
(authority. In the bank reports of some of the States, private banks are) Tj T*
(not distinguished from state banks. This is due to the fact that in) Tj T*
(these States incorporated and unincorporated banks are subject to the) Tj T*
(same regulation. A private bank, however, is an unincorporated bank.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
