(Not all banking institutions incorporated by the States are state banks.) Tj T*
(Mutual savings banks, stock savings banks, and trust companies are also) Tj T*
(corporations organized under state laws or charters granted by state) Tj T*
(legislatures. The distinction between mutual savings banks and state) Tj T*
(banks is clear. Mutual savings banks do not have a capital stock and do) Tj T*
(not carry on a discount and deposit business--_i. e._, they do not) Tj T*
(discount commercial paper, and do not receive demand deposits payable on) Tj T*
(check. State banks, on the other hand, have a capital stock and carry on) Tj T*
(a discount and deposit business. Many state banks, however, receive) Tj T*
(also savings deposits. The line of demarcation between state banks and) Tj T*
(stock savings banks is much less definitely marked. Both state banks and) Tj T*
(stock savings banks have a capital stock. Stock savings banks are) Tj T*
(primarily savings banks, and many of them do not do a discount and) Tj T*
(deposit business, but confine themselves to the savings bank business.) Tj T*
(But in several States the distinction between state banks and stock) Tj T*
(savings banks is of the most unsubstantial character, since the stock) Tj T*
(savings banks carry on the business of a commercial bank, receiving) Tj T*
(demand deposits payable on check, and discounting commercial paper.) Tj T*
(Finally, the distinction between state banks and trust companies is not) Tj T*
(exactly the same in any two of the States.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("State banks" then, as the term is used in the following pages, are) Tj T*
(banks of discount and deposit \(as distinguished from savings banks,) Tj T*
(mutual and stock\) incorporated by one of the States or Territories \(in) Tj T*
(contrast with private banks, which are unincorporated, and with national) Tj T*
(banks, which are organized under the national-bank act\).[141]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1860 there were in the United States 1,562 state banks. Owing to the) Tj T*
(repressive influence of the national-bank act, hastened in its effect by) Tj T*
(the 10 per cent. tax on state-bank notes, the number of state banks had) Tj T*
(by 1868 fallen to 247. One result of this decline in the number and) Tj T*
(importance of state banks was the cessation of state banking) Tj T*
(legislation. The old laws regulating state banks of issue were swept) Tj T*
(away by code revisions, or remained obsolete and unchanged on the) Tj T*
(statute books.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The number of state banks began to increase about 1870. In a few States) Tj T*
(old banking laws intended for the regulation of banks of issue hampered) Tj T*
(their development, but in the remaining States they were left for a) Tj T*
(considerable period almost entirely without regulation. As late as 1892,) Tj T*
(in his digest of the state statute law, Mr. Stimson said:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     It seems unnecessary to incorporate the state banking laws) Tj T*
(     in this edition. Nearly all the States, except the newer) Tj T*
(     States and Territories, have special chapters in their) Tj T*
(     corporation acts concerning banks and moneyed institutions,) Tj T*
(     but these chapters are usually of old date, and have) Tj T*
(     practically been superseded for so long a time by the) Tj T*
(     national banking laws that they have become obsolete in use) Tj T*
(     and form.) Tj T*
