(to the regulation of the state banks has been partly due to the rapid) Tj T*
(growth of the banks in numbers and in financial importance; but it is to) Tj T*
(be accounted for primarily by a change of view as to the purpose of) Tj T*
(banking regulation. The antebellum state-bank regulations were intended) Tj T*
(to secure the safety of the bank note. Although the depositor was) Tj T*
(protected by many of the regulations, this protection was purely) Tj T*
(incidental. The view that note-issuing banks alone required governmental) Tj T*
(regulation persisted for a considerable time after the passage of the) Tj T*
(national-bank act. Since the national banks had a monopoly of the issue) Tj T*
(of bank notes, the regulation of state banks was considered needless. As) Tj T*
(the importance of note issue as a banking function decreased, banking) Tj T*
(regulation, as seen in the national-bank act, began to be considered) Tj T*
(desirable as a protection to depositors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE EVOLUTION OF THE TRUST COMPANY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With the exception of the power to issue notes, which would be) Tj T*
(unavailable because of the tax on note issue, the powers of the state) Tj T*
(banks of to-day are essentially the same as the powers of the state) Tj T*
(banks which were in operation before the Civil War. On the other hand,) Tj T*
(the trust company is a new type of banking institution, the functions of) Tj T*
(which are even yet not clearly defined. A great part of the legislation) Tj T*
(with reference to trust companies, therefore, has had to do with) Tj T*
(defining the powers of these corporations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The early laws for the incorporation of trust companies show the widest) Tj T*
(differences of opinion with regard to their field of operation. The one) Tj T*
(point of agreement appears to have been the idea that a corporation) Tj T*
(could administer trusts more advantageously and safely than an) Tj T*
(individual. But the companies in all the States were given additional) Tj T*
(powers more or less closely connected with their trust powers. Some of) Tj T*
(the companies, chiefly the very early ones, were empowered to insure) Tj T*
(lives and to grant annuities. In a considerable number of States the) Tj T*
(companies were authorized to insure the fidelity of persons in positions) Tj T*
(of trust and in some States to insure titles to land. Almost all the) Tj T*
(companies were empowered to do a safe-deposit business. Among these) Tj T*
(powers there was a certain apparent connection. The power to insure the) Tj T*
(fidelity of trustees, administrators, and executors seemed a natural) Tj T*
(addition to the powers of a company which might act in such capacities.) Tj T*
(Similarly, it appeared that the business of insuring titles to land was) Tj T*
(one which could be most economically conducted by a corporation which,) Tj T*
(in its capacity of trustee, would be a large owner of real estate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One other power was given to practically all the companies--the power to) Tj T*
(receive deposits of money in trust. The following quotation from the) Tj T*
(Report of the Massachusetts Commissioners of Savings Banks for 1871) Tj T*
(shows the use which it was expected would be made of this power:) Tj T*
