(The development of the trust company as reflected in the legislation) Tj T*
(with reference to its powers shows two main tendencies: \(1\) The) Tj T*
(companies have to a very large extent given up the insuring of the) Tj T*
(fidelity of persons in positions of trust and the guaranteeing of land) Tj T*
(titles. \(2\) They have largely increased their banking activities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. In some States which formerly authorised trust companies to insure) Tj T*
(the fidelity of persons in positions of trust, or to guarantee titles to) Tj T*
(real estate, the more recent laws do not permit the combination of such) Tj T*
(business with the business of a trust company.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The fidelity insurance business during the past twenty years has been) Tj T*
(largely concentrated in the hands of a comparatively small number of) Tj T*
(companies which have agencies in all parts of the country and which do) Tj T*
(not undertake a trust or banking business. The elimination of fidelity) Tj T*
(insurance from the functions of the trust company has not been chiefly) Tj T*
(or even largely due to adverse legislation, but to the nature of the) Tj T*
(fidelity insurance business. The most successful conduct of that) Tj T*
(business appears to require, like other kinds of insurance, that the) Tj T*
(risks shall be numerous and widely distributed. These conditions are) Tj T*
(best met by companies which carry on business in many different places.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For the most economical conduct of the title insurance business an) Tj T*
(expensive plant is necessary. The business in each city tends therefore) Tj T*
(to fall into the hands of a single company, which ordinarily finds it) Tj T*
(profitable to devote itself entirely to the one kind of business. At the) Tj T*
(present time, only a very small part of the trust companies in the) Tj T*
(United States insure titles to land.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. The second great tendency in the development of the powers of the) Tj T*
(trust company--the enlargement of its banking powers--has also been) Tj T*
(primarily an economic development and not one due to legislative design.) Tj T*
(As has already been noted, the early trust companies ordinarily had) Tj T*
(power to receive trust deposits and to loan money. Some such powers were) Tj T*
(necessary for the exercise of their trust functions. The opportunity to) Tj T*
(enlarge the banking powers of the companies lay in the difficulty of) Tj T*
(distinguishing clearly between the powers which it was intended to) Tj T*
(confer upon the trust companies and the banking powers possessed by) Tj T*
(state and national banks.) Tj T*
