(incorporation of state banks the amount of capital is thus graded. The) Tj T*
(grading of the amount of capital required according to the population of) Tj T*
(the place in which a bank is located has been chiefly due to the desire) Tj T*
(to bring about some adjustment between the capital of each bank and the) Tj T*
(volume of its business. It is assumed that the larger the business of) Tj T*
(the bank the greater the chance of its suffering large losses and the) Tj T*
(larger the capital necessary to protect its depositors against loss. It) Tj T*
(is also assumed that the size of the city in which it is located is a) Tj T*
(rough index of the volume of business done by a bank. Under many of the) Tj T*
(state banking laws the grades are very numerous. The minute gradation of) Tj T*
(the capital requirements found in many of the state banking laws is due) Tj T*
(to the desire to encourage the formation of banks in the smaller cities) Tj T*
(and towns, for it is to be noted that in the greater part of the state) Tj T*
(laws the grades are not numerous for the larger places.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
