(CHAPTER XXI) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE CANADIAN BANKING SYSTEM) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
([147]Financially, Canada is part of the United States. Fully half the) Tj T*
(gold reserve upon which its credit system is based is lodged in the) Tj T*
(vaults of the New York Clearing House. In any emergency requiring) Tj T*
(additional capital Montreal, Toronto, and Winnipeg call on New York for) Tj T*
(funds just as do St. Paul, Kansas City, and New Orleans. New York) Tj T*
(exchange is a current and universal medium in Canada and is in constant) Tj T*
(demand among the banks. A Canadian wishing to invest in securities that) Tj T*
(may be quickly marketed commonly turns to the New York market for stocks) Tj T*
(and bonds. Yet the American banker visiting in Canada, if he is) Tj T*
(unacquainted with the history of banking in his own country, finds) Tj T*
(himself in a land of financial novelties, for Canada has a banking) Tj T*
(system unlike any in operation in the United States at the present time.) Tj T*
(Twenty-nine banks, known as the "chartered banks," transact all the) Tj T*
(banking business of the Dominion. They have 2,200 branches, and each may) Tj T*
(establish new branches without increase of its capital stock. [At the) Tj T*
(close of the year 1915 there were twenty-two banks with approximately) Tj T*
(3,200 branches.] They issue notes without depositing security with the) Tj T*
(Government and in such abundance that no other form of currency in) Tj T*
(denominations of $5 and above is in circulation. Notwithstanding the) Tj T*
(fact that the notes are "unsecured," their "goodness" is unquestioned) Tj T*
(among the Canadian people.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE SYSTEM NOT NEW) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But to the student of the history of banking in the United States there) Tj T*
