(whenever the notes which the bank has outstanding do not amount to the) Tj T*
(paid-up capital of the bank together with the amount of legal-tender) Tj T*
(money deposited with the trustees. In other words, the banks can still) Tj T*
(issue their notes up to the full amount of their paid-up capital, and an) Tj T*
(additional amount from September 1 to the end of the following February,) Tj T*
(which may equal 15 per cent. of a bank's combined capital and surplus.) Tj T*
(It is only for notes issued in excess of these amounts that legal-tender) Tj T*
(money must be deposited with the trustees at Montreal. It should be) Tj T*
(observed, however, that the banks pay a tax of 4 per cent. on the extra) Tj T*
(issue during the crop-moving period, whereas there is no tax upon) Tj T*
(gold-reserve notes. And as Canadian banks are not required to keep a) Tj T*
(legal reserve against their demand liabilities, there is no reason why) Tj T*
(the idle gold in their reserves should not be sent to Montreal to form) Tj T*
(the basis of new note issues, especially when it is considered that the) Tj T*
(gold may be recalled at once when no longer needed to cover notes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The ability to issue notes to any amount required, on a gold basis, will) Tj T*
(greatly strengthen the position of the banks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The third important new feature in the revision of the act is the power) Tj T*
(given to the banks to make loans to farmers on grain which is stored on) Tj T*
(the farm and still in the farmer's possession.... The permission granted) Tj T*
(them to loan money to farmers on stored grain in the latter's possession) Tj T*
(is an attempt to extend to the farmers aid similar to that hitherto) Tj T*
(granted to manufacturers and wholesalers alone. It should not be) Tj T*
(thought, however, that the banks have not always granted loans liberally) Tj T*
(to farmers....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The possibility of making advances to the farmers on their grain is) Tj T*
(expected to be of especial benefit to the West.... It is hoped that,) Tj T*
(under the new legislation, the farmer will be able to hold his grain for) Tj T*
(higher prices; and in the meantime secure accommodation from the banks) Tj T*
(to meet his obligations. Many bankers, however, refuse to see any remedy) Tj T*
(for the situation in the new legislation. They maintain that it will) Tj T*
(involve too much risk to extend loans on grain over which the farmer) Tj T*
(continues to assert control. Only the operation of time will enable us) Tj T*
(to estimate the value of this feature of the act.) Tj T*
