() Tj T*
([150]About the year 1691 the Government of William and Mary experienced) Tj T*
(considerable difficulty in raising the necessary funds to prosecute the) Tj T*
(war with France; but "the hour brings the man." The man on this occasion) Tj T*
(was William Paterson, a merchant of Scotland, who had been educated for) Tj T*
(the Church, but had led a varied and adventurous life. The scheme he) Tj T*
(presented for the consideration of the Government for the relief of the) Tj T*
(situation was the foundation of a public joint-stock bank; which, in) Tj T*
(return for certain powers and privileges to be conferred, should advance) Tj T*
(money to the Government....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(... the bill establishing the Bank of England was successfully carried) Tj T*
(through Parliament, and obtained the royal assent on the 25th April,) Tj T*
(1694.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The basis of the bill was that L1,200,000 should be voluntarily) Tj T*
(subscribed by the public, and that the subscribers should be) Tj T*
(incorporated into a body, to be known as "The Governor and Company of) Tj T*
(the Bank of England.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The whole of the sum forming the capital of the bank was to be lent to) Tj T*
(the Government, for which the bank was to receive interest at the rate) Tj T*
(of 8 per cent. per annum, together with an allowance of L4,000 per annum) Tj T*
(for management and expenses; making in all L100,000 per annum. It was) Tj T*
(also provided that the sum of L300,000 was to be raised by public) Tj T*
(subscription, for which the contributors were to receive certain) Tj T*
(terminable annuities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By its first charter, which was for ten years only, the Bank of England) Tj T*
(was not allowed to borrow or owe more than the amount of its capital;) Tj T*
(which meant that it could issue notes to the extent of its capital and) Tj T*
(no more. If this amount were exceeded the members were liable for such) Tj T*
(excess, in their private capacities, in proportion to their holding of) Tj T*
(stock.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The capital of the bank was subscribed in a few days, and when duly paid) Tj T*
(up, the agreed sum of L1,200,000 was handed in to the Exchequer....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The charter originally granted to the bank was for ten years only, as we) Tj T*
(have already seen; but this charter has from time to time been renewed,) Tj T*
(and also varied--sometimes in favour of the bank and sometimes) Tj T*
(curtailing its privileges. The monopoly of joint-stock banking was not) Tj T*
(granted to the bank by its first charter, but this monopoly was) Tj T*
(practically conferred on it in 1708. The act passed in that year) Tj T*
(provides:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     That during the continuance of the said corporation of the) Tj T*
(     Governor and Company of the Bank of England, it shall not be) Tj T*
(     lawful for any body politic or corporate whatsoever, created) Tj T*
(     or to be created \(other than the said Governor and Company) Tj T*
(     of the Bank of England\), or for any other persons) Tj T*
(     whatsoever, united or to be united in covenants or) Tj T*
