(     partnership, exceeding the number of six persons, in that) Tj T*
(     part of Great Britain called England, to borrow, owe, or) Tj T*
(     take up any sum or sums of money on their bills or notes,) Tj T*
(     payable at demand, or at a less time than six months from) Tj T*
(     the borrowing thereof....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We pass on now to the end of the eighteenth century, when the country) Tj T*
(was plunged into the throes of war and financial difficulty. Up to this) Tj T*
(time the bank, since its foundation, had succeeded in meeting its notes) Tj T*
(when presented; but in the year 1796 a steady drain on the reserve of) Tj T*
(the bank commenced, owing to the fear of invasion. This drain began to) Tj T*
(assume a very serious aspect in the early part of 1797, and it appeared) Tj T*
(probable that the bank would be subjected to the danger and humiliation) Tj T*
(of a temporary stoppage. The directors, fully aware of this danger ahead) Tj T*
(of them, laid the position before the Government, and left the solution) Tj T*
(of the difficulty in its hands. After due consideration, an Order in) Tj T*
(Council was issued on the 26th February, 1797, requiring the bank not to) Tj T*
(pay its notes in gold.... It was not until 1823 that the restriction was) Tj T*
(entirely withdrawn, although as a matter of fact the bank really) Tj T*
(resumed paying in cash on demand on May 1, 1821, deeming it then safe to) Tj T*
(do so.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Although a period of safety and prosperity then appeared to have dawned,) Tj T*
(the bank was not quite clear of its troubles. The very prosperity of the) Tj T*
(times led imperceptibly to another period of distress and danger,) Tj T*
(culminating in the panic of 1825....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1826 the Bank of England, by arrangement with the Government, agreed) Tj T*
(to establish branches in various parts of the country, and gave up their) Tj T*
(monopoly of joint-stock banking, except within a radius of sixty-five) Tj T*
(miles of London.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The year 1833, however, saw a further restriction in the powers of the) Tj T*
(bank, when, after protracted negotiations, and in return for a further) Tj T*
(renewal of its charter, the bank surrendered its monopoly of joint-stock) Tj T*
(banking entirely, provided that no bank having more than six partners) Tj T*
(might issue notes within the sixty-five-mile limit of London.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is a curious point that the charter of the bank never did restrict) Tj T*
(joint-stock banking in its present accepted form, but only the issue of) Tj T*
(notes by joint-stock bankers or banks having more than six partners. Up) Tj T*
(to this time the issue of notes by a bank had been thought to be its) Tj T*
(main business; so much so, that it was believed to be useless to attempt) Tj T*
(to conduct a bank without power of issue, and consequently no) Tj T*
(joint-stock bank had been founded. But about this time the need of such) Tj T*
(institutions began to be felt, and the presumed monopoly of the Bank of) Tj T*
(England was called in question--largely by Mr. Gilbart, the founder of) Tj T*
(the London and Westminster Bank. The bank tried to assert their) Tj T*
(monopoly, but without success, and in order to settle the matter) Tj T*
(effectually, the following clause was inserted in the act passed in 1833) Tj T*
(dealing with the bank charter:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
