() Tj T*
(The main point of contention between the supporters and opponents of the) Tj T*
(act lies in its want of elasticity in time of need. Under no) Tj T*
(circumstances can the bank increase its issue of notes against) Tj T*
(securities beyond the prescribed limit, without a breach of the law; but) Tj T*
(on three occasions in the past the law has been broken, though with the) Tj T*
(consent of the Government, and subsequent confirmation of Parliament....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We will now briefly review the ... occasions on which the Bank Act was) Tj T*
(suspended, and the effect of such suspensions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first of these occasions was during the panic in the year) Tj T*
(1847--known as the "railway panic." Shortly previous to this year a) Tj T*
(great accumulation of capital had led to a demand for new investments,) Tj T*
(which were duly provided for the public by those concerned with such) Tj T*
(matters. Added to this, interest rates had ruled low for some time, and) Tj T*
(this conduced to a period of speculative activity. Too much capital was) Tj T*
(put into fixed investments--chiefly railways--and in one session of) Tj T*
(Parliament sanction was asked for various railway schemes involving a) Tj T*
(total capital of L340,000,000. Wild gambling in railway stocks ensued,) Tj T*
(credit was inflated above all reason, and then the turn came. This was) Tj T*
(primarily due to a bad harvest and potato crop, causing a heavy) Tj T*
(importation of corn, and consequent export of gold.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(During the panic which ensued, the reserve of the Bank of England fell) Tj T*
(to L1,600,000, but when the panic was at its height, the act, passed) Tj T*
(only three years before, was suspended. The bank was authorised to) Tj T*
(increase its accommodation to the public by exceeding, to an indefinite) Tj T*
(extent, the limit fixed for the issue of notes not secured against gold.) Tj T*
(The effect of this suspension of the act was immediate and complete. The) Tj T*
(fear that "there was not enough to go round" passed from men's minds. As) Tj T*
(a matter of fact, the issue on this occasion did not exceed the normal) Tj T*
