(The second suspension of the Bank Act was due to the crisis of 1857, a) Tj T*
(crisis that was brought about by reckless overtrading, and came upon the) Tj T*
(public very suddenly and with practically no warning....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The third suspension of the Bank Act took place in 1866.[152] Many) Tj T*
(elements of disturbance to the money market had been in force during two) Tj T*
(or three preceding years. The Civil War in America had resulted in gold) Tj T*
(being sent to this country; but the stoppage of the supply of cotton) Tj T*
(from America, owing to the war, disorganised one of our staple national) Tj T*
(industries, and supplies of cotton had to be obtained from elsewhere at) Tj T*
(high prices, and paid for in cash. Hence a drain of gold set in on a) Tj T*
(large scale. In addition, a large speculation had been built up on) Tj T*
(credit in the stocks and shares of the many new limited liability) Tj T*
(companies which were formed at that time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(General uneasiness began to prevail towards the end of 1865; in January,) Tj T*
(1866, the bank raised its discount rate to 8 per cent., and a crisis) Tj T*
(began to develop rapidly....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the 9th May the bank rate was raised to 9 per cent. On the 10th May) Tj T*
(the failure of Overend, Gurney, and Company--for upwards of ten) Tj T*
(millions--was announced, and the bank rate went to 10 per cent. This) Tj T*
(failure was not made known till after business hours, so it was not till) Tj T*
(Friday, the 11th May, 1866--known as "Black Friday"--that the crisis) Tj T*
(reached its height.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The stoppage of this large house affected the whole world, and general) Tj T*
(failure seemed imminent, when, in the afternoon of the day on which the) Tj T*
(failure became known, it was announced that the Bank Act was again) Tj T*
(suspended, and calm began to take the place of mania. But though the) Tj T*
(panic was allayed, many failures shortly took place, which delayed the) Tj T*
(quick restoration of a sense of security....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From the above brief records of the financial tragedies of the past, we) Tj T*
(see that on each occasion reckless speculation and overtrading had been) Tj T*
(allowed to reach a dangerous height before any steps were taken to check) Tj T*
(them, and on each occasion the check came too late. But we also see the) Tj T*
(marvellously quick effect which the suspension of the act had on the) Tj T*
(situation....) Tj T*
