(the cheque can be turned into cash. The cheque can be made payable to A) Tj T*
(B or bearer, and in this form can be taken to the bank drawn on and) Tj T*
(immediately turned into cash. When drawn to A B or order, a cheque has) Tj T*
(to be indorsed, or signed on the back, by A B before the bank drawn on) Tj T*
(will pay it. A still further restriction is the English system of) Tj T*
(crossing cheques, that is to say, of drawing two lines across the face) Tj T*
(of the cheque, by which mark it is shown that the cheque is not to be) Tj T*
(paid in cash across the counter by the bank drawn on, but must be paid) Tj T*
(into a bank by the payee, and so only becomes credited to him in his own) Tj T*
(banking account through the operations of the clearing house. It is) Tj T*
(evident that this protection greatly increases the safety of the cheque,) Tj T*
(since if it fell into the wrong hands its chance of being made) Tj T*
(fraudulent use of is greatly diminished. As the lines drawn across the) Tj T*
(face of the check by the bankers' customers are often faint and) Tj T*
(irregular, it has been found in practice that they lend themselves to) Tj T*
(the ingenuity of the fraudulent, who are easily enabled to erase them) Tj T*
(and so obtain possession of money that is not meant for them. Some of) Tj T*
(the banks therefore print these crossing lines on all of the cheques) Tj T*
(that they issue to their customers to be filled in, and when the) Tj T*
(customer wishes to obtain cash from his bank on one of these cheques he) Tj T*
(is consequently obliged to write upon it "Please pay cash," and sign) Tj T*
(this note upon it. The extensive use of crossed cheques thus tends to) Tj T*
(make the cheque still further an instrument which merely transfers) Tj T*
