() Tj T*
(Let me illustrate their method of working: A bank in New York may buy) Tj T*
($1,000,000 worth of sterling bills drawn on England and send them) Tj T*
(forward to its London agent to be discounted with the bill broker. The) Tj T*
(bill broker will discount these bills at, say, 4 per cent. If he thinks) Tj T*
(rates are likely to fall, he will hold the bills; if he thinks them) Tj T*
(likely to rise, he will try to sell the bills at about 3-3/4 per cent.) Tj T*
(or 3-7/8 per cent. discount, thus making a profit on the transaction of) Tj T*
(1/4 per cent. or 1/8 per cent. per annum. Similarly he may discount) Tj T*
(large parcels of bills for Eastern and South American banks. Many of) Tj T*
(these bills will be bills drawn on and accepted by banks and finance) Tj T*
(houses. These are known as "bank bills." But on the other hand, the bill) Tj T*
(brokers are free buyers of "trade bills." The trade bill in England) Tj T*
(arises in the following way: Trader A sells goods to trader B. He will) Tj T*
(draw a draft on trader B at, say, three months date. Trader B will) Tj T*
(accept the draft and return it to trader A, who will discount it with) Tj T*
(his banker or with the bill broker. The rate of discount for trade bills) Tj T*
(is usually 1/2 per cent. per annum higher than the rate for bank bills.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The essential feature of almost all the bills on the market is that they) Tj T*
(represent a commercial transaction, such as a sale of goods, where value) Tj T*
(passes. It is this that lends them their self-liquidating quality; for) Tj T*
(they are usually liquidated by the acceptor out of the proceeds of the) Tj T*
(resale of the goods during the currency of the bill.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
