(THE SCOTCH BANKS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
([158]The functions performed by the eight Scotch banks and their 1,245) Tj T*
(branches[159] are essentially similar to those already described as) Tj T*
(being carried out by their English brethren. The differences between the) Tj T*
(currency systems of the two countries are in degree rather than in) Tj T*
(essence. In Scotland the note issue has made a harder fight for its) Tj T*
(existence than in England, owing no doubt to the fact that the Bank of) Tj T*
(England's monopoly did not extend to Scotland and that the great Scotch) Tj T*
(joint-stock banks therefore extended the system of using notes as) Tj T*
(currency, while the development of joint-stock banking in England was) Tj T*
(necessarily opposed to it, since joint-stock banks in England with an) Tj T*
(office in London were unable to issue notes. Nevertheless, even in) Tj T*
(Scotland the advantages of the cheque have told in its favour, and, as) Tj T*
(will be seen below, liabilities of Scotch banks under note issue are now) Tj T*
(much smaller than those under deposit as current accounts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(DEMOCRACY OF SCOTCH BANKING) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Scotch note circulation increased from L5,332,000 in 1872 to) Tj T*
(L7,173,000 in 1908. This increase, when compared with the fact that the) Tj T*
(note issues of the English country banks have during the same period) Tj T*
(diminished almost to vanishing point, shows that the bank note is much) Tj T*
(more tenacious of life north of the Tweed. This is partly owing to the) Tj T*
(fact that in Scotland notes may be issued of the denomination of L1,) Tj T*
(whereas in England the smallest allowed is of L5, so that the note was) Tj T*
(thus circulated more easily among the poorer classes in Scotland and so) Tj T*
(gained and retained a hold upon a much wider circle of the community. In) Tj T*
(this respect, as in others, Scotch banking is more democratic than) Tj T*
(English, and provides its facilities for a poorer and lower class of the) Tj T*
(community, though this distinction between the banking systems of the) Tj T*
(two countries is being rapidly diminished. Especially in its early days) Tj T*
(it laid itself out much more readily to the encouragement of the small) Tj T*
(capitalist and borrower, often granting him facilities against security,) Tj T*
(or an absence of security, which would have been only regarded as) Tj T*
(feasible under quite exceptional circumstances in England. A very) Tj T*
(interesting system was at one time fairly general in Scotland, and is) Tj T*
(even now by no means obsolete. It was the system described as that of) Tj T*
(cash credits, by which borrowers were able to go to banks and obtain) Tj T*
(advances against the joint personal security of themselves and one, or) Tj T*
(two, or three friends. By this means, in which a kind of co-operative) Tj T*
(responsibility was recognised as a security by the Scotch bankers, very) Tj T*
(poor borrowers were enabled to obtain banking facilities, and many) Tj T*
(instances are recorded in which by a loan of this kind, of quite small) Tj T*
(importance from the banking point of view, foundations of fortunes have) Tj T*
(been laid and the general commercial prosperity of the community has) Tj T*
(been furthered in a very satisfactory manner. And even now the essential) Tj T*
(difference between Scotch and English banking is this readiness of the) Tj T*
(former to take into consideration the personal standing of the applicant) Tj T*
