(USE OF NOTES AS "TILL MONEY" IN RELATION TO THE ESTABLISHMENT OF) Tj T*
(BRANCHES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Banking by branches in Scotland has proceeded even more rapidly than in) Tj T*
(England, and the percentage of branches per head of the population is) Tj T*
(higher in the northern part of the Kingdom. This wide diffusion of) Tj T*
(banking facilities in Scotland has been largely brought about by the) Tj T*
(fact that its banks, having the privilege of note issue, were able to) Tj T*
(hold their own notes as "till money," so economising in the matter of) Tj T*
(cash. The following passage is from a work entitled _Scottish Banking,) Tj T*
(1865-1896_, by A. W. Kerr, author of a _History of Banking in Scotland_:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     Were it not for the power to issue notes, and the readiness) Tj T*
(     with which the public receive them, the banks could never) Tj T*
(     have afforded to open a third of the branches which have) Tj T*
(     been established. The reason for this is a very simple one.) Tj T*
(     Without the right of issue a bank must, at every one of its) Tj T*
(     offices, hold the whole of its balance of cash in the shape) Tj T*
(     of coin, or of notes of other banks, which, as far as it is) Tj T*
(     concerned, are as unprofitable as coin. Such balances entail) Tj T*
(     a complete loss of interest which can only be borne where) Tj T*
(     the amount of business is of considerable extent. There are) Tj T*
(     probably not above 100 \(at most 200\) localities in Scotland) Tj T*
(     that would satisfy such conditions. When, however, a bank) Tj T*
(     can hold its till money in the shape of notes, it is enabled) Tj T*
(     to extend its operations into districts which would) Tj T*
(     otherwise be quite inaccessible....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The authority of a practical Scotch banker is equally emphatic on the) Tj T*
(point. Mr. Robert Blyth, general manager of the Union Bank of Scotland,) Tj T*
(read a paper at the thirty-first annual convention of the American) Tj T*
(Banking Association, in October, 1905, on the subject of Scottish) Tj T*
(banking. In the course of this very interesting paper he made the) Tj T*
(following statement: "It is in another quarter altogether that the) Tj T*
(Scotch banks find the value of the L1 note. It is the unissued notes in) Tj T*
(the tills of the branch offices, forming the till money at more than a) Tj T*
(thousand branches, wherein the real value lies. Without them the banks) Tj T*
(would require to keep L8,000,000 or L10,000,000 of gold coin, not as a) Tj T*
(reserve but as till money. It is these L1 notes which have enabled) Tj T*
(branch offices to be planted in every part of the country.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It thus appears, from the highest possible authority, that the Scotch) Tj T*
(banks are enabled by their right of note issue to economise gold to the) Tj T*
(extent of L8,000,000 or L10,000,000, and it is amusing to observe how) Tj T*
(the objects aimed at by Peel's legislation with regard to note issue) Tj T*
(have thus been defeated even more completely in Scotland than in) Tj T*
(England. In England banking turned the flank of Peel's Act by developing) Tj T*
(the use of cheques, which superseded the note as the common form of) Tj T*
