(EVASION OF PEEL'S ACT) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Scotland used the same weapons as England, namely, the cheque and the) Tj T*
(development of deposit banking. The eight Scotch banks have, according) Tj T*
(to their latest balance sheets, L7,000,000 of notes outstanding, and) Tj T*
(L108,000,000 of liability on deposits and drafts. With regard to the) Tj T*
(latter item Peel's regulations had nothing to say, and since ordinary) Tj T*
(banking prudence demanded that some cash should be held against it, and) Tj T*
(since the gold held against notes was not specially earmarked as such,) Tj T*
(Scotch banking was able to treat its cash against deposits as the basis) Tj T*
(both of its notes and deposits and so produce the economy which is) Tj T*
(boasted of by its champions. The law says nothing concerning cash to be) Tj T*
(held against deposits, and the metallic basis of these is probably) Tj T*
(extremely slender, if the cash held against notes is set on one side;) Tj T*
(but it is impossible to detect its actual amount, since the Scotch banks) Tj T*
(include with their cash their balances at the Bank of England, etc. And) Tj T*
(the net result is, that when the proportion of its cash to its total) Tj T*
(liabilities on notes and deposits is worked out it is found to be) Tj T*
(decidedly low, even when compared with English practice. For the eight) Tj T*
(banks taken together, gold and silver coin, notes of other banks, cash) Tj T*
(at Bank of England, and cheques in course of transmission represent) Tj T*
(almost exactly 10 per cent. of their note and deposit liabilities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It should be observed that the notes which the Scotch banks hold as till) Tj T*
(money do not appear in their statements, for until they are issued they) Tj T*
(are not a liability, and though they are treated by the banks in) Tj T*
(practice as an asset, they can not figure as such in a balance sheet.) Tj T*
(That they are practically treated as such is witnessed by Mr. Blyth, as) Tj T*
(quoted above, when he says that without them the banks would require to) Tj T*
(keep L8,000,000 or L10,000,000 of gold coin. And it is, of course, this) Tj T*
(habit of regarding unissued notes as a banking asset in the shape of) Tj T*
(till money that accounts for the low reserve of actual cash that the) Tj T*
(Scotch banks show.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(DEFECTS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Scotch banking is so generally regarded as one of the highest) Tj T*
(achievements of the banking intelligence that some hesitation is natural) Tj T*
(in criticising the system by which, according to its own evidence, it) Tj T*
(has obtained most of its success. At the same time, it is difficult to) Tj T*
(avoid the conclusion that a serious danger lurks in a system which) Tj T*
(regards a banker's unissued promise to pay in the light of a banking) Tj T*
(asset. Mr. Blyth points out that these unissued notes are "not a reserve) Tj T*
(but till money," but the distinction between till money and reserve is) Tj T*
