(weakened, for it need not be said that in case of serious trouble, which) Tj T*
(is a contingency of which all provident bankers have at all times to) Tj T*
(beware, a bank's own promises to pay would be of little service to it.) Tj T*
(If a bank's credit were doubted, these promises to pay would not be) Tj T*
(available for it in meeting demands upon it. At such periods the public) Tj T*
(requires from its bankers not promises to pay but physical gold. In) Tj T*
(Scotland the confidence of the public in its bankers is so great, and) Tj T*
(the readiness with which it circulates their promises to pay appears to) Tj T*
(be so ingrained in the national character, that the contingency of the) Tj T*
(demand of the public for gold seems to be extremely remote. The) Tj T*
(criticism therefore which detects a weak point in this asset upon which) Tj T*
