(The notes are ... legal tender, but, of course, may be exchanged, at) Tj T*
(sight, against cash--I don't say against gold, as I will explain) Tj T*
(presently.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The denominations circulating at present are $10, $20, $100, and $200.) Tj T*
(One dollar and $4 notes were issued at critical times, but have been) Tj T*
(withdrawn since. In case of need, they would be resorted to again, and) Tj T*
(in this respect I should like to mention the fact, demonstrated by) Tj T*
(experience, that even where the circulation is already sufficient, a) Tj T*
(supplementary issue of small notes--unless, of course, the amount be too) Tj T*
(unreasonable--is much less likely to depreciate the currency than an) Tj T*
(issue of larger ones. In a certain sense, we may consider that a country) Tj T*
(which refrains from issuing small notes in normal times, possesses _ipso) Tj T*
(facto_ a valuable reserve in case of emergency....) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I need not recall the remarkable role played by the Bank of France,) Tj T*
(under the leadership of its very distinguished governor, M. Pallain,) Tj T*
(during critical periods such as 1907, when that institution succeeded in) Tj T*
(keeping the French discount rate on an exceptionally moderate level,) Tj T*
(while giving valuable and effective aid, at the same time, to the London) Tj T*
(market.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(How is this successful policy of the Bank of France materially possible?) Tj T*
