(for that of the general credit-seeking members.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Unfortunately this natural difference was elevated, or rather dragged) Tj T*
(down, into an issue of principle; and the law of 1889, drawn up under) Tj T*
(the guidance of the Schulze-Delitzsch party, insisted that every) Tj T*
(co-operative society should have shares. The Raiffeisen societies comply) Tj T*
(with this by nominal shares of \(say\) 10 marks[189] on which no dividend) Tj T*
(is declared; though, occasionally, some of the annual profit is) Tj T*
(indirectly returned to individuals in the shape of a slight addition to) Tj T*
(deposit rates and a slight deduction from loan charges, calculated at) Tj T*
(the end of the year.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Because Raiffeisen wished to create credit among small agriculturists) Tj T*
(out of the immaterial asset of mutual knowledge, he limited the size of) Tj T*
(each society to a single village. For his purpose he was right, but his) Tj T*
(partisans are not right when they look askance at the larger areas of) Tj T*
(the town bank, where the nature of the members' business and the) Tj T*
(society's control is different.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All profits remain the collective property of the society, to be used) Tj T*
(for the society's good. They are divided into two classes of reserve) Tj T*
(fund--\(1\) reserve fund proper; \(2\) foundation fund. The former is) Tj T*
(regulated in the same way as in town banks. The second corresponds to) Tj T*
(the shareholders' dividend. It is undesirable to have nothing beyond an) Tj T*
(ordinary reserve fund, because money thus placed can only be withdrawn) Tj T*
(to cover losses: while if placed in the foundation fund it can be used) Tj T*
(for positive improvements, such as the extension of premises or the) Tj T*
(establishment of a burial fund. In actual figures, the reserve funds are) Tj T*
(not so strong as in the town bank, owing in part to the lower loan) Tj T*
ET
