(charges.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The loan capital, as in the town banks, is made up of small savings and) Tj T*
(deposits. It is drawn, either from within the area covered by the bank,) Tj T*
(in which case it comes both from members and non-members, the former) Tj T*
(being where possible rewarded at slightly higher rates in order to) Tj T*
(encourage membership; or from without the area, in which case it of) Tj T*
(necessity comes from non-members. Savings are received in sums from one) Tj T*
(mark upwards: the smaller amounts being collected by penny stamp books,) Tj T*
(similar to those used in the Post Office Savings banks of England. The) Tj T*
(willingness with which the peasants bring their savings to the bank is a) Tj T*
(triumphant proof of Raiffeisen's contention that the small) Tj T*
(agriculturists by a combination of unlimited liability and close) Tj T*
(supervision can become absolutely credit-worthy. No savings since the) Tj T*
(foundation of the first village bank have ever been lost through) Tj T*
(bankruptcy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In addition the bank obtains credit from a central bank with which it) Tj T*
(has a current account.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The funds thus raised are utilised for three kinds of credit--\(1\) Simple) Tj T*
(loans; \(2\) current accounts; \(3\) property transfers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Current accounts are rare except in villages where there is a little) Tj T*
(industry. With regard to the simple loan, the security, as in town) Tj T*
(banks, is personal pledge, land mortgage, or \(very rarely\) deposit of) Tj T*
(collateral. The personal pledge, as with Schulze-Delitzsch, is the most) Tj T*
(frequent. But Raiffeisen interpreted it more strictly than) Tj T*
(Schulze-Delitzsch. Not only must the credit-seeker produce an outside) Tj T*
(testimony to his character: he must also convince his society that he) Tj T*
(really merits this testimony. The member of the Schulze-Delitzsch bank) Tj T*
(is accepted on the strength of his general business reputation, added to) Tj T*
(his security, personal or material. The member of the Raiffeisen bank,) Tj T*
(though he have the best of pledges, is rejected unless he is known in) Tj T*
(his private life to be virtuous and industrious. The man of doubtful) Tj T*
(sobriety has no chance of obtaining anything from a country bank.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If it happen that an applicant is little known or new in the district,) Tj T*
(so that no one will go pledge for him, then the society, provided it is) Tj T*
(convinced of his good character, will grant a loan against land) Tj T*
(mortgage. This is not to be confused with the real credit granted by a) Tj T*
(land bank, where the value of the estate alone is considered. It is) Tj T*
(personal credit with a material caution, and it is not a long-term loan.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Furthermore, the society requires to know not only the character of the) Tj T*
(borrower, but also the specific object for which his loan is destined.) Tj T*
(It must be satisfied not only that the borrower wishes to employ the) Tj T*
(loan in his business, but also that the operation proposed is likely to) Tj T*
(turn out successful.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Property transfers are not strictly credit business. They are in the) Tj T*
(nature of investments for superfluous money, just as a town bank might) Tj T*
