(invest in railway shares, with the difference that the investment is) Tj T*
(local and designed to meet indirectly the credit wants of members. The) Tj T*
(nature of the operation is as follows: A dies, leaving his estate to his) Tj T*
(heirs; and these, perhaps because they wish to leave the neighbourhood) Tj T*
(or because they want ready money for other reasons, put up the estate) Tj T*
(for sale in allotments. Or perhaps A during his lifetime wishes to get) Tj T*
(rid of a part of his estate. X, Y, Z, neighbouring peasants, are buyers,) Tj T*
(but they can pay only gradually--which they are allowed to do by law.) Tj T*
(The credit bank steps in as intermediary. It pays to the heirs of A or) Tj T*
(to A himself, as the case may be, the price of the estate minus a small) Tj T*
(commission. X, Y, Z become the debtors of the credit society, paying off) Tj T*
(their debt by regular instalments, which include principal and interest.) Tj T*
(The bank cuts out small traffickers in land, usually Jews, to the) Tj T*
(benefit of sellers and buyers. It benefits the sellers by charging them) Tj T*
(a moderate instead of an extravagant commission: the buyers by saving) Tj T*
(them from permanent relationship with land dealers who seek their ruin.) Tj T*
(The bank insists on regular payment of the instalments, because it wants) Tj T*
(its money back, while the dealer is constantly tempting the buyers to) Tj T*
(fall into arrears in order that he may eventually acquire the land) Tj T*
(himself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is a second form of property transfer, where the bank not only) Tj T*
(acts as intermediary but itself holds the estate for a time. Some land) Tj T*
(dealer, having obtained a mortgage on the estate of A, demands payment.) Tj T*
(A cannot pay and is forced to sell his estate by public auction. The) Tj T*
(dealer forces the sale, just when the estate market is likely to be most) Tj T*
(unfavourable, hoping to buy the estate for himself at an absurdly low) Tj T*
(rate. Thereupon the bank steps in; it bids against the dealer, and if he) Tj T*
(does not offer a good price, buys the estate itself and resells it later) Tj T*
(in the year, when the market is more favourable. In this way A can pay) Tj T*
(off his debts at once. Moreover, the bank does not keep the difference) Tj T*
(between the price of purchase and final resale. After the deduction of) Tj T*
(a moderate commission, it is handed over to A, who thus obtains a) Tj T*
(further sum with which he can make a fresh start.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These dealings in property transfers are confined to Southwest Germany,) Tj T*
(where estates are sold to be split up into little lots. The banks only) Tj T*
(enter on these transactions where the following conditions are) Tj T*
(satisfied--\(a\) where they have a superfluity of money over and above) Tj T*
(that needed in their ordinary loan business; \(b\) where some party to the) Tj T*
(transaction is a member of the society: either the seller or the buyer) Tj T*
(or the creditors of the seller holding second and third mortgages, who) Tj T*
(would obtain nothing were the estate sold below its real value.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
