(What are the results achieved by the rural bank, thus operating and thus) Tj T*
(controlled?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(More than ten times the number of country banks grant only one-sixth of) Tj T*
(the credit afforded by the town banks. The total membership of the) Tj T*
(country banks is nearly twice as large, but the average membership per) Tj T*
(bank is nearly seven times as small.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The average credit advanced per member is 500 marks. The average rate of) Tj T*
(interest is not exactly known; it appears to be between 4 and 5 per) Tj T*
(cent., _i. e._, nearly 1 per cent. cheaper than in the town bank. The) Tj T*
(duration of loans varies between one and ten years in accordance with) Tj T*
(the requirements of agriculture. They are repayable in small) Tj T*
(instalments, covering principal and interest, although the member may) Tj T*
(repay in lump if he wishes. The loan can always be called on four weeks') Tj T*
(notice, but the right is never exercised, unless the borrower is) Tj T*
(allowing his property to deteriorate or is becoming insolvent through) Tj T*
(extravagance or has misapplied money lent for a particular purpose. The) Tj T*
(inculcation of punctuality in payment, as a moral duty, was the hardest) Tj T*
(of Raiffeisen's tasks, as it was his greatest triumph.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If it be asked finally what Raiffeisen banks have done, which other) Tj T*
(banks have not, it may be replied that Raiffeisen created out of) Tj T*
(hopeless chaos the only kind of credit organisation possible for the) Tj T*
(small agriculturist. Industry necessarily brings business men together) Tj T*
(to some extent. Agriculture in itself holds the farmer apart, and) Tj T*
(preserves him in lonely ignorance to be the victim of the perambulating) Tj T*
(money-lender. To-day more than 50 per cent. of the independent) Tj T*
(agriculturists of Germany are members of rural banks; and another 10 per) Tj T*
(cent., chiefly the larger farmers, are members of town banks. The) Tj T*
(non-co-operative agriculturist is becoming the exception. The Raiffeisen) Tj T*
(banks are thickest in the southwest of Germany, the home of the small) Tj T*
(peasant proprietors. Indeed the change wrought in many of these villages) Tj T*
(is nothing short of a revolution. The experience of the parent village) Tj T*
(bank may serve in illustration:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
