(CHAPTER XXVI) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(BANKING IN SOUTH AMERICA) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
([196]The special interest in South American banking which exists at this) Tj T*
(time is the product of at least four distinct factors:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(First. It has been evident for some years that the trade between North) Tj T*
(and South America is rapidly developing. In the ten years, 1903-1913,) Tj T*
(the exports from the United States to the ten Republics of South America) Tj T*
(increased 274 per cent. against an increase of all our exports during) Tj T*
(the same period of 73 per cent. In spite of inexperience, crude methods,) Tj T*
(lack of banks and of ships we have made notable gains in South American) Tj T*
(trade. There seems to be no reason to question the probability of a) Tj T*
(continued rapid increase during the next few years.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(OUR GROWING SURPLUS FOR FOREIGN INVESTMENT) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Second. Other forces have gradually been bringing this country more and) Tj T*
(more into the position of looking for investment opportunities abroad.) Tj T*
(While it is true that the United States is a debtor nation in the sense) Tj T*
(that a large amount \(estimated at $3,000,000,000 to $6,000,000,000\) of) Tj T*
(European capital is invested here, it is also true, on the other hand,) Tj T*
(that the national income has for some years been sufficient to meet) Tj T*
(annual payments abroad, to make large fresh investments in our own) Tj T*
(enterprises, and still to leave a considerable surplus for investment in) Tj T*
(neighbouring countries. It is estimated that American capital in Mexico) Tj T*
(and Canada amounts approximately to $1,500,000,000. In South America) Tj T*
(there are already American investments of perhaps $300,000,000 to) Tj T*
($400,000,000.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As the national income and savings expand and as the opportunities for) Tj T*
(exceptionally profitable investment within this country decrease, it is) Tj T*
(clear that there must be a stronger and stronger tendency toward) Tj T*
(investment abroad. The immense sums, for instance, that have been) Tj T*
(flowing into railroad construction and rebuilding will not be needed to) Tj T*
(so great an extent in future. A considerable proportion of this overflow) Tj T*
(of capital may certainly be expected to spread into South America.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(GREATER LENDING POWER OF BANKS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Third. The adoption of the federal reserve system has made a remarkable) Tj T*
(improvement in the handling of gold and of credit. It has released and) Tj T*
(made available for other forms of financing great sums which were) Tj T*
(formerly tied up in scattered reserves. We have only to look at the) Tj T*
(monetary history of the German Empire during the last forty years to see) Tj T*
