(CONDITIONS OF COMMERCIAL BANKING) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A banking business, like any other, must adapt itself to surrounding) Tj T*
(conditions, including laws, business customs, precedents created by) Tj T*
(older banks, and the like. In South America these conditions differ in a) Tj T*
(number of respects from those which prevail in the United States.) Tj T*
(Probably the first impression of most observers gives an exaggerated) Tj T*
(idea of the differences. However, they should be fully and carefully) Tj T*
(considered.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The chief differences that directly affect banking operations are the) Tj T*
(following: \(1\) Comparative absence of banking regulation on the part of) Tj T*
(governments or associations; \(2\) national colonies; \(3\) social character) Tj T*
(of business relations; \(4\) lack of highly developed economic) Tj T*
(organisation; \(5\) relatively high and stable rates of interest; and \(6\)) Tj T*
(in some countries fluctuating currencies. The first five of these) Tj T*
(circumstances call for brief comment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(LITTLE CONTROL OR CO-OPERATION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Not only is there a marked absence of laws directly applicable to) Tj T*
(banking concerns, but there is also an equally noteworthy absence of) Tj T*
(control exercised either by the Government or by associations among the) Tj T*
(banks. Even the large governmental or semi-governmental banks in Brazil,) Tj T*
(Uruguay, Argentina, Chile, and Bolivia are competitive with the other) Tj T*
(banks. Whatever influence they exercise is secured through their active) Tj T*
(and direct competition, not through any special authority over the other) Tj T*
(banks conferred upon them. In the fall of 1914, for the first time,) Tj T*
(there was some rediscounting of the paper held by other banks on the) Tj T*
(part of the Bank of the Argentine Nation and of the Bank of the) Tj T*
(Republic of Uruguay; but this tendency did not go far. The other banks) Tj T*
(objected to placing information as to their relations with customers in) Tj T*
(the hands of the governmental institutions. In other countries there has) Tj T*
(not been even this much of an attempt toward fulfilling the functions of) Tj T*
(a central bank of rediscount.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is difficult to secure in most of the South American cities even the) Tj T*
(most elementary kind of co-operation among the banking institutions. How) Tj T*
(is it possible that they should continue to stand apart when they would) Tj T*
(obviously gain so much by coming together? A partial answer is to be) Tj T*
(found in the peculiarity that has already been pointed out, namely, the) Tj T*
(fact that many of the more powerful institutions are the offspring of) Tj T*
(European countries. Each one is fighting to support the trade of a) Tj T*
(certain well-defined group of clients. The national antagonisms among) Tj T*
(them are deep-seated and sometimes virulent. All this was true even) Tj T*
(before the European war. It will be tenfold true for a number of years) Tj T*
(to follow.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
