([197]Various European nations, with soil naturally inferior to ours,) Tj T*
(have established agricultural credit and thereby have greatly eased the) Tj T*
(burden of the cost of living. Hitherto we have lived on the bountiful) Tj T*
(overflow of our rich land, and the pinch of necessity has not been felt;) Tj T*
(but now our population has grown enormous, our standards of living have) Tj T*
(been greatly raised, and our land is showing the effect of generations) Tj T*
(of taking out with very little putting back. We must do better or) Tj T*
(suffer.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By the installation of agricultural credit, farming will not only be) Tj T*
(made more profitable, but it will in the end make country life more) Tj T*
(attractive. The banking system of to-day is adapted to the needs of) Tj T*
(manufacture and commerce. The processes of nature are so much slower,) Tj T*
(however, that banking for farmers must be organised on a basis of credit) Tj T*
(for much longer periods.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our present system of borrowing on land is by mortgages running from) Tj T*
(three to five years, the entire principal coming due at one time. This) Tj T*
(is expensive, involving renewals, and dangerous from the possibility of) Tj T*
(the mortgage falling due at a time of restricted credit so that it) Tj T*
(cannot be renewed. On the continent of Europe this business is handled) Tj T*
(by so-called land-mortgage banks, or rather associations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The mortgages granted are pledged for the security of bonds which the) Tj T*
(institution issues and sells in the general market. These bonds have no) Tj T*
(fixed maturity, but can be retired at par or some small premium at any) Tj T*
(time. When the borrower mortgages his land to the bank he agrees to pay) Tj T*
(a certain fixed sum semi-annually. This is called the "annuity" and is) Tj T*
(composed of the annual interest plus an amount, generally 1/2 per cent.,) Tj T*
(toward the reduction of the principal of the debt and known as) Tj T*
("amortisation," and an additional amount, about 1/4 per cent., toward) Tj T*
(the expenses of the bank. The borrower, therefore, at once begins to) Tj T*
(extinguish the principal of the debt; and as each year the principal) Tj T*
(decreases, the interest, of course, decreases also, and, the annuity) Tj T*
(being fixed, the proportion of it applicable toward the extinction of) Tj T*
(the mortgage increases. Thus it happens that, beginning with a payment) Tj T*
(of 1/2 per cent. toward principal, the mortgage bearing 4 per cent. to) Tj T*
