(communities. On September 1, 1911, out of the total 7,301 national banks) Tj T*
(there were 1,966 with a capital of $25,000, and therefore presumably) Tj T*
(located in towns of less than 3,000 population, 372 with a capital) Tj T*
(between $25,000 and $50,000, and therefore presumably in towns of less) Tj T*
(than 6,000 population, and 2,297 with a capital between $50,000 and) Tj T*
($100,000. Except for banks in towns not exceeding 6,000 population, the) Tj T*
(law as amended in 1900 does not permit any national bank to be organized) Tj T*
(with a capital less than $100,000.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Are the national banks which are accessible to farmers in a position) Tj T*
(under the law to meet farmers' needs? The answer to this question must) Tj T*
(be in the affirmative. Aside from the fact that national banks are not) Tj T*
(permitted to make loans on real estate security,[200] there is no) Tj T*
(restriction in the national banking act which would interfere with loans) Tj T*
(to farmers for agricultural purposes. Personal security alone is legally) Tj T*
(acceptable; the range of possible collateral security is practically) Tj T*
(unlimited; and there is no limitation fixed by law as to the period of) Tj T*
(loans. National banks therefore have a very free hand in regard to loans) Tj T*
(to farmers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When we inquire concerning agricultural credit in banks under state) Tj T*
(charters we find conditions varying with the different States, but, with) Tj T*
(a few minor qualifications, it may be said that the state banking laws) Tj T*
(are free from restrictions that would hamper state banks and trust) Tj T*
(companies in extending credit liberally to responsible farmers. They) Tj T*
(are in a much better position in one respect to deal with farmers than) Tj T*
(are national banks, that is, in the matter of accepting real estate) Tj T*
(security. No state denies state banks this privilege, and such) Tj T*
(restrictions as exist upon its exercise are generally not onerous.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If commercial banks are comparatively unhampered by law in making) Tj T*
(short-time loans to farmers, it may be asked: To what extent are such) Tj T*
(loans made? Unfortunately practically no information is available on) Tj T*
(this question. In answer to an inquiry the Comptroller of the Currency) Tj T*
(wrote, under date of May 27 of this year, that no information with) Tj T*
(reference to short-time loans made to farmers by national banks had ever) Tj T*
(been compiled by the comptroller's office. The writer has found no trace) Tj T*
(of any investigation of this subject by state banking departments. For) Tj T*
(about a year he has taken occasion to inquire at every opportunity of) Tj T*
(individual bankers concerning their experience with regard to loans to) Tj T*
(farmers in different parts of the country. The replies received are so) Tj T*
(divergent that no conclusion can be drawn from them, except that the) Tj T*
(practice varies widely in different sections of the country and even in) Tj T*
(different communities in the same section, and that probably the farmers) Tj T*
(of the North Central and Western States borrow of commercial banks more) Tj T*
(than do those of the Eastern and Southern States. There is not) Tj T*
(sufficient evidence, however, for this latter inference to make it much) Tj T*
(more than a guess. In the absence of any comprehensive data, I shall) Tj T*
(resort to the unsatisfactory but representative replies from different) Tj T*
(parts of the country.) Tj T*
ET
