(Another interesting suggestion comes from Irving Fisher. By statistics) Tj T*
(he has shown that when for any reason prices begin to rise, interest) Tj T*
(rates advance, but not fast enough to offset the decline in the) Tj T*
(purchasing power of the principal caused by the rise of prices. During) Tj T*
(such periods, accordingly, borrowers on the whole get the better of) Tj T*
(lenders and make high profits. Since the borrowers consist largely of) Tj T*
(active business men, precisely the class of greatest foresight, they) Tj T*
(grasp the situation more quickly than lenders. As a result of their) Tj T*
(desire to profit by their opportunity, loans are rapidly extended. This) Tj T*
(extension is effected largely by the lending of bank credits, that is,) Tj T*
(by the increasing of deposit currency. The greater volume of the) Tj T*
(currency combines with more rapid circulation of money and checks to) Tj T*
(increase prices again, and so to start the whole process anew on a) Tj T*
(higher level. "There is thus set up a vicious circle, which will) Tj T*
(continue just as long as the rate of interest fails to make a proper) Tj T*
(adjustment to put on the brakes and prevent over-borrowing.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
("But the rise in interest, though belated, is progressive, and, as soon) Tj T*
(as it overtakes the rate of rise in prices, the whole situation is) Tj T*
(changed." Borrowers can no longer hope to make great profits, and the) Tj T*
(demand for loans ceases to expand. Further, the higher rate of interest) Tj T*
(reduces the price of many of the securities used as collateral for) Tj T*
(loans. Business men "who have counted on renewing their loans at the) Tj T*
(former rates and for the former amounts are unable to do so. It follows) Tj T*
(that some of them are destined to fail." There follow suspicions) Tj T*
