() Tj T*
(As prosperity approaches its height, then, a sharp contrast develops) Tj T*
(between the business prospects of different enterprises. Many, probably) Tj T*
(the majority, are making more money than at any previous stage of the) Tj T*
(business cycle. But an important minority, at least, face the prospect) Tj T*
(of declining profits. The more intense prosperity becomes, the larger) Tj T*
(grows this threatened group. It is only a question of time when these) Tj T*
(conditions, bred by prosperity, will force some radical readjustment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now such a decline of profits threatens worse consequences than the) Tj T*
(failure to realize expected dividends. For it arouses doubt concerning) Tj T*
(the security of outstanding credits. Business credit is based primarily) Tj T*
(upon the capitalized value of present and prospective profits, and the) Tj T*
(volume of credits outstanding at the zenith of prosperity is adjusted to) Tj T*
(the great expectations which prevail when the volume of trade is) Tj T*
(enormous, when prices are high, and when men of affairs are optimistic.) Tj T*
(The rise of interest rates has already narrowed the margins of security) Tj T*
(behind credits by reducing the capitalized value of given profits. When) Tj T*
(profits themselves begin to waver the case becomes worse. Cautious) Tj T*
(creditors fear lest the shrinkage in the market rating of the business) Tj T*
(enterprises which owe them money will leave no adequate security for) Tj T*
(repayment. Hence they begin to refuse renewals of old loans to the) Tj T*
(enterprises which cannot stave off a decline of profits, and to press) Tj T*
(for a settlement of outstanding accounts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thus prosperity ultimately brings on conditions which start a) Tj T*
(liquidation of the huge credits which it has piled up. And in the course) Tj T*
(of this liquidation prosperity merges into crisis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
