(STRINGENT MONEY AND FINANCIAL PANICS[247]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Is there any tendency for financial panics to occur more frequently in) Tj T*
(the seasons of the year when the money market is normally stringent? It) Tj T*
(has been found that the two periods of the year in which the money) Tj T*
(market is most likely to be strained are the periods of the spring trade) Tj T*
(revival \(about March and April\) and that of the crop-moving demand in) Tj T*
(the fall; and that the two periods of the easiest money market are the) Tj T*
("readjustment period," extending from about the middle of January to) Tj T*
(about the first of March, and the period of the summer depression,) Tj T*
(extending through the summer months. Of the eight panics which have) Tj T*
(occurred since 1873, four occurred in the fall or early winter \(_i. e._,) Tj T*
(those of 1873, 1890, 1899, and 1907\); and one \(_i. e._, that of 1903\)) Tj T*
(extended from March until well along in November. Out of a total of) Tj T*
(twenty-one minor panics or "panicky periods" occurring between 1876 and) Tj T*
(1908, inclusive, nine occurred during the fall and early winter, eight) Tj T*
(during the spring, one began in May and extended into June, three) Tj T*
(occurred during the summer months, and one occurred in February. The) Tj T*
(evidence accordingly points to a tendency for the panics to occur during) Tj T*
(the seasons normally characterized by a stringent money market.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
