(be the consequence if the wise mayor of a town should attempt to meet) Tj T*
(the danger of fire by distributing the available water, giving each) Tj T*
(house owner one pailful? When the lightning strikes, the unfortunate) Tj T*
(householder will in vain fight the fire with his one pailful of water,) Tj T*
(while the other citizens will all frantically hold on to their own) Tj T*
(little supply, their only defence in the face of danger. The fire will) Tj T*
(spread and resistance will be impossible. If, however, instead of) Tj T*
(uselessly dividing the water, it had remained concentrated in one) Tj T*
(reservoir with an effective system of pipes to direct it where it was) Tj T*
(wanted for short, energetic, and efficient use, the town would have been) Tj T*
(safe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We have parallel conditions in our currency system, but, ridiculous as) Tj T*
(these may appear, our true condition is even more preposterous. For not) Tj T*
(only is the water uselessly distributed into 21,000 pails, but we are) Tj T*
(permitted to use the water only in small portions at a time, in) Tj T*
(proportion as the house burns down. If the structure consists of four) Tj T*
(floors, we must keep one-fourth of the contents of our pail for each) Tj T*
(floor. We must not try to extinguish the fire by freely using the water) Tj T*
(in the beginning. That would not be fair to the other floors. Let the) Tj T*
(fire spread and give each part of the house, as it burns, its equal and) Tj T*
(inefficient proportion of water. _Pereat mundus, fiat justitia!_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(REDEPOSITED OR OVERLAPPING RESERVES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([254]If we are to understand the radical change which will be worked by) Tj T*
(the Federal Reserve Act in the reserve situation in this country it is) Tj T*
(necessary to examine at some length the system heretofore prevailing.) Tj T*
(Under the National Bank Act these banks were divided into three groups) Tj T*
(or classes, referred to as the country banks, the reserve city banks and) Tj T*
(the central reserve city banks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are three central reserve cities: New York, Chicago, and St.) Tj T*
(Louis. Every national bank in these cities is a central reserve city) Tj T*
(bank. The reserve cities are forty-seven in number and include the) Tj T*
(larger cities of the country. Every bank not situated in any one of the) Tj T*
(three central reserve cities or the forty-seven reserve cities is a) Tj T*
(country bank. This last term includes all the national banks of the) Tj T*
(smaller cities in the country, of the manufacturing towns and) Tj T*
(communities of New England and the Middle States and thousands of) Tj T*
(national institutions doing business in the agricultural sections.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(~The Country Banks.~--The country banks, by the terms of the National Bank) Tj T*
(Act, are required to keep a cash reserve at all times equal to 15 per) Tj T*
(cent. of their deposits. Under the old law the country bank must keep) Tj T*
(only 40 per cent. of this required reserve in its own vaults, while it) Tj T*
(is allowed to deposit 60 per cent. of the required reserve on call in) Tj T*
