() Tj T*
(The demand for currency depends upon the volume of business to be) Tj T*
(transacted, and is continually in a state of fluctuation. Various causes) Tj T*
(have only to be mentioned to explain the unequal demand at different) Tj T*
(times. We have thus the payments of salaries, bills, etc., coming) Tj T*
(usually, on the first of each month. Then there are the quarterly) Tj T*
(payments of dividends, interest, etc., falling generally on the first of) Tj T*
(January and at intervals of three months thereafter during the year.) Tj T*
(Above all, we have in this country a regularly recurring seasonal change) Tj T*
(in the volume of business, due to the harvesting and moving of the crops) Tj T*
(every fall and early winter. Besides these normal fluctuations in the) Tj T*
(demand for currency there are of course such abnormal circumstances as) Tj T*
(business emergencies, panics, depressions, etc., which at irregular) Tj T*
(intervals call for expansion or contraction of the currency. To meet all) Tj T*
(these varied demands an elastic currency is a necessity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The most serious evils of inelasticity in this country are seen in) Tj T*
(connection with the annual handling of the crops. It may be safely said) Tj T*
(that for this purpose the United States needs every fall at least one) Tj T*
(hundred and fifty million dollars of extra currency. Since our monetary) Tj T*
(system contains no really elastic element, this extra business of the) Tj T*
(fall has to be done with little or no increase of the country's) Tj T*
(currency. The crops must be handled by means of a shifting of currency) Tj T*
(from one part of the country to another. In the spring and early summer) Tj T*
(the agricultural districts are apt to have more money than they need.) Tj T*
(Accordingly, the country banks are in the habit of depositing part of) Tj T*
(their reserves in banks situated in the reserve cities. A large part of) Tj T*
(these sums eventually finds its way into the money markets of New York) Tj T*
(and other Eastern cities, where a low rate of interest is paid to) Tj T*
(outside banks for such deposits. Now comes the harvest season, and a) Tj T*
(demand goes up from the country banks for the return of their deposits.) Tj T*
(Every fall the clearing-house banks of New York City alone give up about) Tj T*
(fifty millions of "lawful money" to meet this demand.[256] Of course) Tj T*
(this means a tight money market. In the spring and summer the funds) Tj T*
(obtained from the country banks were loaned out or used as reserves for) Tj T*
(deposits. Money was in excess, interest rates were low, and speculation) Tj T*
(was encouraged. Now loans must be called in and deposits reduced. This) Tj T*
(sudden contraction is a hard blow to all business interests. It is) Tj T*
(especially hard on the speculators, and their desperate demands cause) Tj T*
(the enormous rates on call loans which are witnessed every fall on the) Tj T*
(New York money market....) Tj T*
