(It is thus seen that the popular idea that the holder of a national bank) Tj T*
(note is secured against loss by the government bonds deposited in) Tj T*
(Washington is not strictly correct. What protects the holder of a note) Tj T*
(is the absolute responsibility of the Treasury to redeem all notes on) Tj T*
(demand. The bonds are to secure the Treasury, not the individual) Tj T*
(noteholder, against loss. The noteholder is secured so long as the) Tj T*
(Treasury is able to meet its legal obligations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Let us examine the character of our government bonds as security to) Tj T*
(enable the Treasury to meet its obligations. To understand the) Tj T*
(situation, it should be remembered that the leading purpose in the) Tj T*
(establishment of the national banking system was not the creation of a) Tj T*
(scientific currency system. The National Bank Act was a war measure) Tj T*
(enacted largely for the purpose of improving the market for government) Tj T*
(bonds during the Civil War. It was for this purpose that the circulation) Tj T*
(of state banks was forced out of existence by a 10 per cent. tax and the) Tj T*
(right of issue restricted to national banks on condition of the deposit) Tj T*
(of government bonds as security. In the accomplishment of this purpose) Tj T*
(the act has been eminently successful. United States bonds have been) Tj T*
(given a new utility over and above their utility as an investment. From) Tj T*
(the very beginning, this has given them an added value and enabled the) Tj T*
(Government to borrow at lower rates of interest than it would otherwise) Tj T*
(have had to pay. The act of March 14, 1900, made provision for the) Tj T*
(ultimate refunding of all the United States debt into 2 per cent. bonds,) Tj T*
(and gave an added inducement to the use of these bonds as note security) Tj T*
(by lowering the annual tax on circulation from 1 per cent. to one-half) Tj T*
(of 1 per cent., provided the notes were secured by the new 2 per cent.) Tj T*
(bonds. All bonds issued since 1900 have borne 2 per cent. interest. Yet) Tj T*
(the market value of these bonds has always stood above par....) Tj T*
(Obviously, this value is not based on earnings. British consols paying) Tj T*
(2-1/2 per cent. are to-day quoted in the neighborhood of 85, which makes) Tj T*
(them yield about 3 per cent. on the investment. The French and German 3) Tj T*
(per cent. loans are both considerably below par. United States bonds) Tj T*
(have been given an artificial value through their use as security for) Tj T*
