() Tj T*
([259]When a banker takes out currency he engages in two distinct) Tj T*
(transactions and enters upon two different hazards. In one transaction) Tj T*
(he assumes the risk and holds the expectation of greater profit for) Tj T*
(taking out circulation. Since buying bonds and taking out circulation) Tj T*
(most of the time shows some theoretical profit over loaning direct,) Tj T*
(presumably if there were no other consideration, most of the time our) Tj T*
(bankers would keep outstanding all the notes they could. In the other) Tj T*
(transaction, however, the banker engages in a speculation in government) Tj T*
(securities. As a matter of fact, if the price of government bonds) Tj T*
(advances, the profit from taking out circulation declines; but our) Tj T*
(banker is pretty likely to view with equanimity the declining) Tj T*
(circulation profit when he considers the profit he is making in his) Tj T*
(speculation in bonds. On the other hand, as the price of government) Tj T*
(bonds declines, circulation grows more profitable. The banker is likely) Tj T*
(to view this with sour satisfaction when he looks on his loss in his) Tj T*
(bond speculation. Profit or loss in the bond speculation is likely to) Tj T*
(outbalance loss or profit in the circulation transaction.[260]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
