(THE LACK OF ADJUSTMENT BETWEEN BANK NOTES AND DEPOSITS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([261]Under our present currency system the volume of money in) Tj T*
(circulation is perfectly flexible. It constantly expands and contracts) Tj T*
(in automatic adjustment to the requirements of trade and the convenience) Tj T*
(of the people. An increase in the volume of cash transactions brings) Tj T*
(promptly an increase in the volume of currency in circulation through) Tj T*
(the current withdrawals of money exceeding the current deposits of) Tj T*
(money. A lessening in the volume of cash transactions promptly drives) Tj T*
(unneeded currency out of circulation through the deposits of money) Tj T*
(exceeding the withdrawals. No other system could provide a currency) Tj T*
(which would adjust its volume in circulation more exactly to the needs) Tj T*
(of trade and the preferences of the people. There is a ceaseless flow of) Tj T*
(the money in circulation into bank reserves, and of money in bank) Tj T*
(reserves into circulation--ceaseless except in an occasional crisis when) Tj T*
(the natural flow of money from bank reserves into circulation is) Tj T*
(arbitrarily stopped by banks refusing, for self-protection, to continue) Tj T*
(paying out to the point of exhausting reserves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(While the volume of money in circulation is thus perfectly and) Tj T*
(automatically adjusted to trade requirements, it is to be noted that) Tj T*
(this flexibility arises from the flow back and forth, between the mass) Tj T*
(of money in circulation and the mass in bank reserves. In this lies the) Tj T*
(main economic defect of our present currency system. An expansion in the) Tj T*
(volume of money in circulation entails a corresponding contraction in) Tj T*
(the volume of bank reserves, and necessarily a corresponding contraction) Tj T*
