(THE FEDERAL RESERVE ACT[287]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE SPIRIT AND OBJECTS OF THE ACT) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The primary purpose of the Federal Reserve Act of December 23, 1913, is) Tj T*
(to make certain that there will always be an available supply of money) Tj T*
(and credit in this country with which to meet unusual banking) Tj T*
(requirements. Banks of a new class, to be known as Federal Reserve) Tj T*
(Banks, are to be established, and upon these banks is to rest the heavy) Tj T*
(responsibility of supporting the structure of credit in periods of) Tj T*
(financial strain. The new banks are expected to keep themselves in a) Tj T*
(condition of such strength in ordinary times that the other banks may) Tj T*
(safely rely upon them for all needed cash and credit in emergencies. In) Tj T*
(the past, the banks in this country, when subjected to financial) Tj T*
(pressure, have relied mainly upon loan contraction and the selling of) Tj T*
(securities. In future it is expected that they will resort to the) Tj T*
(Federal Reserve Banks, securing additional funds from these by) Tj T*
(rediscounting commercial loans. If the new arrangements work well, loans) Tj T*
(in future will not be reduced merely for the purpose of strengthening) Tj T*
(the banks. Loan contraction will take place only when there is evidence) Tj T*
(of an over-extended condition of business; and even then contraction) Tj T*
(will be carried through gradually, so as to conserve all interests so) Tj T*
(far as may be possible. Under the new system a most important influence,) Tj T*
(if not the most important single influence determining the character of) Tj T*
(banking operations, will be just the reverse of what it has been in the) Tj T*
(past.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To meet the heavy responsibilities placed upon the Federal Reserve) Tj T*
(Banks, two things are absolutely essential--good management, and ample) Tj T*
