() Tj T*
(The various considerations which led to the adoption of the plan for) Tj T*
(regional banks, rather than a single central institution, deserve) Tj T*
(careful attention, since they indicate the spirit and purpose of the) Tj T*
(Federal Reserve Act. A single central bank was the solution of the) Tj T*
(banking problem reached without a dissenting voice by the members of the) Tj T*
(National Monetary Commission. The bill which the commission prepared was) Tj T*
(a notable achievement. Pioneer work though much of it necessarily was,) Tj T*
(very few defects on the technical banking side were disclosed in the) Tj T*
(discussion which followed the statement of the proposed measure. Its) Tj T*
(provisions regarding banking operations, including relations with other) Tj T*
(banks, are embodied with few changes of an essential character in the) Tj T*
(Federal Reserve Act. Most of the important differences between the bill) Tj T*
(and the Federal Reserve Act reflect differences in spirit and purpose) Tj T*
(rather than in methods. A central bank and also the system of regional) Tj T*
(banks necessarily involve placing somewhere very extensive power to) Tj T*
(influence and control credit. In the present temper of public opinion,) Tj T*
(the possession of great economic power is not tolerated in the absence) Tj T*
(of a large measure of government supervision and control. But) Tj T*
(unfortunately, in framing its measure the monetary commission failed to) Tj T*
(realize the fundamental importance of this consideration as a factor in) Tj T*
(securing general public approval. In devising a form of organization,) Tj T*
(competent management and approval in banking circles were evidently the) Tj T*
(controlling factors. An organization was proposed under which out of) Tj T*
(forty-five directors, but three were to represent the Government, the) Tj T*
(remainder being selected in various ways by bankers. Support from some) Tj T*
(who were the most bitter opponents of the measure might have been) Tj T*
(secured if the bill had provided for a larger measure of government) Tj T*
(control; but an equal or even greater number of adherents would probably) Tj T*
(have been lost. Under the plan of the commission and indeed under any) Tj T*
(central bank plan, government supervision and control cannot be made) Tj T*
(effective without at the same time placing the details of operation in) Tj T*
(charge of government officials. Few of the most ardent advocates of a) Tj T*
(central bank were prepared to take this extreme step.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Under the plan of organization of regional banks, the difficulty of) Tj T*
(combining government control and private management vanished. Purely) Tj T*
(banking matters, such as the granting of loans, could be placed with) Tj T*
(boards entirely or mainly composed of persons selected by the bankers) Tj T*
(whose funds were to provide most of the necessary resources. On the) Tj T*
(other hand, supervision and whatever measure of control might be deemed) Tj T*
(advisable, could be placed with a board mainly or entirely appointed by) Tj T*
(the President of the United States. Differences of opinion may be) Tj T*
(entertained regarding the particular arrangements in the Federal Reserve) Tj T*
(Act for selecting the various administrative bodies, and regarding the) Tj T*
(division of power between the directorates of the federal reserve banks) Tj T*
(and the Federal Reserve Board. If experience should disclose defects in) Tj T*
(this form of organization, it is flexible enough to permit at any time) Tj T*
(an extension of government or of banking influence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another important advantage of the regional system is to be noted. The) Tj T*
(operation of a central bank would be far more likely to give rise to) Tj T*
