(sectional antagonism. This danger was apparently fully realized by the) Tj T*
(members of the National Monetary Commission, and elaborate arrangements) Tj T*
(for selecting the management were devised in order to make certain that) Tj T*
(each section of the country should be properly represented. But) Tj T*
(obviously regional banks, managed by local people, are very much more) Tj T*
(certain to meet this requirement. Apparently it was an endeavor to) Tj T*
(remove still further the danger of sectional dissatisfaction that led) Tj T*
(the Monetary Commission to make its one serious departure from sound) Tj T*
(banking principle in framing its bill. A provision was inserted) Tj T*
(requiring rediscounts to be made at a uniform rate throughout the entire) Tj T*
(country, regardless of the wide differences in the demand and supply of) Tj T*
(capital, which occasion the existing wide differences in lending rates.) Tj T*
(Under the regional plan no such indefensible provision was found) Tj T*
(necessary. This important feature of the Federal Reserve Act outweighs) Tj T*
(such advantages in economy of resources and effectiveness in management) Tj T*
(as were sacrificed in substituting for a central bank the regional) Tj T*
(banks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Monetary Commission in framing its bill seems to have been guided by) Tj T*
(two principles generally wise in legislation--the scope of the measure) Tj T*
(was limited to the single purpose of removing purely banking defects in) Tj T*
(our banking system, and no greater departure from existing arrangements) Tj T*
(was proposed than was essential for the purpose in hand. The Federal) Tj T*
(Reserve Act certainly runs counter to the first of these principles. Its) Tj T*
(primary purpose is similar to that of the bill of the monetary) Tj T*
(commission; but a secondary purpose evidently exercised a potent) Tj T*
(influence. This purpose was to decentralize credits by lessening the) Tj T*
(concentration of banking funds in a few large banks in the chief) Tj T*
(financial centers, and especially in New York. The regional system) Tj T*
(itself gained much support because it was believed by many that it would) Tj T*
(lessen the financial predominance of New York City. No comprehensive) Tj T*
(scheme of legislation with this object in view was inserted in the bill;) Tj T*
(but wherever two or more means of accomplishing the primary purpose of) Tj T*
(the bill were open, that one was evidently selected which it was) Tj T*
(believed might tend toward decentralization. In general the desire to) Tj T*
(decentralize credits explains why the act makes very much greater) Tj T*
(changes in existing arrangements than were proposed in the bill of the) Tj T*
(Monetary Commission. In the latter, the practice of depositing a part of) Tj T*
(the required reserves of the banks with reserve agents was left) Tj T*
(undisturbed. Under the terms of the Federal Reserve Act, such deposits) Tj T*
(are to be reduced by successive installments, and discontinued entirely) Tj T*
(three years after the passage of the act. From a purely banking point of) Tj T*
(view, much can be said for this great change; but it was certainly not) Tj T*
(absolutely necessary in order to secure the desired improvements in the) Tj T*
(working of our banking system.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The new banking institutions for which the Federal Reserve Act makes) Tj T*
(provision cannot be put in successful operation \(and in this it) Tj T*
(resembles the bill of the Monetary Commission\) unless a considerable) Tj T*
(number of the existing banks enter into relations with them. An) Tj T*
(institution might have been established with large capital, and a) Tj T*
(monopoly of the right of note issue, authorized to act as government) Tj T*
