(fiscal agent, and to deal with the general public. Such an institution) Tj T*
(would presumably in the course of time have become a central bank, the) Tj T*
(main reliance of other banks in emergencies. In order to avoid) Tj T*
(competition with existing banks, the act provides that the receipt of) Tj T*
(deposits by the Federal Reserve Banks, and their normal lending) Tj T*
(operations shall be confined to those banks which subscribe to the) Tj T*
(capital and maintain balances with them. Obviously, then, if banks in) Tj T*
(large numbers do not accept the arrangement, subscribing to the capital) Tj T*
(and relying upon the new banks for accommodation, the system cannot be) Tj T*
(put into effective operation. Moreover, it is necessary that many banks) Tj T*
(shall enter the system at the outset. An attitude of hesitation would) Tj T*
(change to one of positive distrust, if the initial response were) Tj T*
(inadequate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the case of the bill of the Monetary Commission, reliance was placed) Tj T*
(simply upon the attractiveness of the measure. No bank would have) Tj T*
(suffered positive loss from failure to enter the system, though certain) Tj T*
(slight inducements were held out to those banks which accepted the) Tj T*
(arrangement at the outset. Whether a sufficient number of banks would) Tj T*
(have entered that system, if it had been established, may be thought) Tj T*
(probable but is not certain. Bankers are naturally and properly a) Tj T*
(conservative class and the inclination of many would have been to wait) Tj T*
(until the system was in successful operation. The attitude of bankers) Tj T*
(toward the Federal Reserve Act while it was passing through Congress was) Tj T*
(distinctly unfavorable. Most of its provisions already referred to, as) Tj T*
(well as others in which it differed from the Monetary Commission bill,) Tj T*
(were disliked. It was evident that in the absence of positive pressure,) Tj T*
(the number of banks which would accept its terms would be too small to) Tj T*
(make successful operation possible. No attempt was made, however, to) Tj T*
(insert provisions which would bring pressure upon state banking) Tj T*
(institutions. Perhaps it would be possible, either under the inter-state) Tj T*
(commerce or the postal clause in the Constitution; but it would have) Tj T*
(been contrary to the constitutional traditions of the party in power,) Tj T*
(and it was not necessary. If the national banks very generally enter the) Tj T*
(system, the resources of the Federal Reserve Banks will be sufficient to) Tj T*
(test the effectiveness of the measure. Accordingly the Federal Reserve) Tj T*
(Act contains a number of provisions designed to bring pressure to bear) Tj T*
(upon these to enter the system immediately. Failure to accept the terms) Tj T*
(of the act within one year after its passage involves forfeiture of the) Tj T*
(national charter. This alone would be no great business sacrifice, since) Tj T*
(banking in most States is quite as profitable under a state as under a) Tj T*
(national charter. Loss of the national charter, however, involves a loss) Tj T*
(of the right to issue bank notes and calls for the deposit of lawful) Tj T*
(money in Washington equivalent to the amount of outstanding circulation.) Tj T*
(Most national bank notes are secured by 2 per cent. government bonds,) Tj T*
(the price of which, in the absence of the circulation privilege, would) Tj T*
(be perhaps about two-thirds of the price \(somewhat above par\) at which) Tj T*
(they were purchased by the banks. No considerable number of national) Tj T*
(banks could refuse to enter the system without involving themselves in a) Tj T*
(heavy immediate loss. A further provision in the act puts more immediate) Tj T*
(pressure upon the national banks in reserve cities. If within sixty days) Tj T*
(after the passage of the act, a reserve agent bank fails to signify) Tj T*
