(winter of 1893-94. Nevertheless, it can hardly be doubted that, after) Tj T*
(even an incipient panic, there will be some reaction, and consequently a) Tj T*
(more or less plethoric condition of the currency will follow. Will the) Tj T*
(new issue have sufficient contractility to meet this need? Earlier in) Tj T*
(this paper we have seen that the conditions attached to the new issue) Tj T*
(are in general not favorable to contractility, in that they do not) Tj T*
(provide for either the prompt driving home or the prompt drawing home of) Tj T*
(the notes when the necessity for their issue is past. Outsiders lack) Tj T*
(adequate motives for sending the notes home; issuers lack adequate) Tj T*
(motives for calling them home. The case for emergency contractility,) Tj T*
(however, is somewhat better than the case for ordinary contractility.) Tj T*
(First, it is probable that the homing power of the note will prove) Tj T*
(greater at such a time than in an ordinary year, for, at such a time,) Tj T*
(outside banks will not be able to find investments for their funds,) Tj T*
(since speculative trading will disappear altogether and business) Tj T*
(generally will be at a very low ebb. Again, it seems certain that the) Tj T*
(issuing bank will, in this case, have more than the usual motive for) Tj T*
(bringing about a contraction of the circulation. The chief reason why) Tj T*
(such a bank may not be eager in ordinary times to hasten the retirement) Tj T*
(of its notes is the fact that, provided the notes do not accumulate in) Tj T*
(its own vaults, such a bank will gain more by using the funds in its) Tj T*
(possession to make loans than it would by using them to retire notes,) Tj T*
(assuming that the interest charge made by the Federal Reserve Board is) Tj T*
(not placed excessively high. But it is practically certain that, in the) Tj T*
(depression which follows a panic, no reserve bank will have) Tj T*
(opportunities for keeping all of its funds busy; and since, in that) Tj T*
(case, the interest charge, however small, will be a dead loss, the bank) Tj T*
(will have adequate motive for effecting, as promptly as possible, an) Tj T*
(adequate contraction of its note liabilities. This motive would be still) Tj T*
(further strengthened should the glut prove sufficient to cause a decided) Tj T*
(drain of gold, since, in that case, the reserve banks will find) Tj T*
(difficulty in maintaining the required 40 per cent. reserve. On the) Tj T*
(whole, then, we seem warranted in affirming that, as respects emergency) Tj T*
(elasticity, the new notes will give no serious disappointment.) Tj T*
