() Tj T*
(It will be noted that not all of the flows described are examples of the) Tj T*
(_circulation_ of money. As already indicated, money may be said to) Tj T*
(circulate only when it passes in exchange for _goods_. Its entrance into) Tj T*
(and exit from banks is a flow, but not a circulation against goods. In) Tj T*
(the diagram the horizontal arrows represent such mere banking) Tj T*
(operations, not true circulation. On the other hand, the arrows along) Tj T*
(the sides of the triangle represent actual circulation. The diagram) Tj T*
(shows four such arrows, representing the four chief types of) Tj T*
(circulation: O_{c} payments of money from "other depositors" to) Tj T*
("commercial depositors" in the purchase of goods; O_{o} payments from) Tj T*
("other depositors" to "nondepositors," as when a housewife pays wages;) Tj T*
(C_{n} payments from "commercial depositors" to "nondepositors," as when) Tj T*
(a firm pays wages; and N_{c} payments from "nondepositors" to) Tj T*
("commercial depositors," as when a wage earner buys goods of a merchant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There four types of circulation of money occur in the three circuits) Tj T*
(already described, being sandwiched between the flows from and to the) Tj T*
(banks. The first, O_{c}, is contained within the circuit B_{o} O_{c}) Tj T*
(C_{b} and, since no "nondepositors" intervene, represents money changing) Tj T*
(hands once between its withdrawal from bank and its redeposit there. The) Tj T*
(remaining types \(O_{n}, C_{n}, and N_{c}\) are contained within the two) Tj T*
(other circuits \(B_{o} O_{n} N_{c} C_{b} and B_{c} C_{n} N_{c} C_{b}\),) Tj T*
(and, owing to the fact that "nondepositors" intervene, represent money) Tj T*
(circulating twice between withdrawal and redeposit.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In short, one of the three circuits \(B_{o} O_{c} C_{b}\) shows money) Tj T*
(circulating once out of bank. Both the others pass through N, and show) Tj T*
(money circulating twice out of bank. The diagram, then, represents all) Tj T*
(circulating money as springing from and returning to the banks; all of) Tj T*
(it as circulating at least once in the interim; and that portion handled) Tj T*
(by "nondepositors" as circulating once in addition. Therefore, the total) Tj T*
(circulation exceeds the total flow from and to banks by the amount) Tj T*
(flowing through "nondepositors." In other words, the total circulation) Tj T*
(in the diagram is simply the sum of the annual money flowing from and to) Tj T*
(banks and the money handled by "nondepositors." The quotient of this sum) Tj T*
(divided by the amount of money in circulation will give approximately) Tj T*
(the velocity of circulation of money....) Tj T*
